AB  - From the high school restroom to the university water tower, graffiti surrounds the daily life of students, and as a kind of unconventional billboard frequently created by their own peer group, attracts their attention. Accordingly, a "little c" cultural lesson that utilizes this source of authentic language to implement the National Standards taps natural appeal. As language teachers travel in the target culture and snap photographs of well-known sites and typical scenes that portray daily life, they may actually position their lenses to avoid unsightly spray paint scrawls rather than envisioning them as teaching tools. This article demonstrates that the use of graffiti can be a truly academic exercise that reaches the goals set forth in the National Standards. A procedure for the lesson is suggested, samples of actual graffiti are given, and possible extensions of the topic are provided.
