AB  - In July 1998 1 had the opportunity to examine the Poggio Sommavilla flask, which is currently held in Boston's Museum of Fine Arts. My autopsy of the flask(1) has brought to light new elements that may suggest a new epigraphic edition of its text. The vulgata of the text is generally based on second-hand reproductions that have greatly influenced its reading. This condition is also partly due to the temporary loss of the flask itself, and its subsequent reappearance in places which are rather difficult to reach. The article in question is a small flask with a double-convex sided body in which all the surfaces have been indistinctly used as an epigraphic support. Thus, the graffiti is distributed in three sections, corresponding to the neck (a), and the two opposite sides (b and c). For a better understanding of the facts, it is well to begin with a brief summary of the text's editorial history.
