TY  - JOUR
TI  - Private and public: Links between symposion and syssition in fifth-century Athens
AU  - Steiner, A
T2  - CLASSICAL ANTIQUITY
AB  - Although the symposium in ancient Athens has been the subject of much scholarly attention for the past twenty years, there has been far less attention paid to dining in syssitia at public expense in the Athenian Agora. This article surveys what we do know about public dining and the extent to which it can be described as "elite" or "democratic." The primary focus is a deeper analysis of some well-known evidence from one Athenian fifth-century syssition dating to around 460 BCE. The date of the evidence coincides with a period of tense negotiation between mass and elite over roles and influence in the workings of Athenian government, and is therefore of particular interest. Results reveal several surprising areas of clear overlap between communal drinking by archons in this public space and what we reconstruct for elite symposia held by members of drinking groups in their own houses. Common features include the naming of kalos individuals, the use of red-figure pottery, the appearance of an apparent love-gift, references to women and their love-interests, and abusive name- calling. Some graffiti inscriptions preserved on the pottery fragments suggest that archons may have engaged in explicit parodies of institutions intended to limit elite power and promote accountability: ostracism and public inscriptions. Because the archonships were limited to the two property-owning classes at the time of the evidence, elites are credited with carryover of the identified props and behaviors from private to public venues. The analysis explores the extent to which dining at this establishment can be called "democratic."
