TY  - CONF
TI  - ISTANBUL PRIDE WEEK AND MARCH; PROTEST ART AS PERFORMANCE
AU  - Sahin, AS
T2  - PERFORMING ARTS AND PUBLIC SPACE, PERFORMART '14
A2  - Seyben, BY
A2  - Kalem, A
A2  - Gungor, A
AB  - Protest Art, generally has been associated various disciplines (but not limited to) like site specific installations, graffiti, street art, performance etc, which all together entwine, merge and create an unique and independent form of art. Among all this maybe performance is the most well known, appraised and accepted discipline. Music, acting, collaboration, artistic creation etc are in the fabric of performance. Thus it makes performance and protest art closely linked with each other.
Istanbul, has been hosting LGBTI Pride Week for over 22 years now. During the last 12 years LGBTI Pride March has been organised as the final event of the Pride Week. Both Pride Week and march, respectively for more than two decades and one, have been platforms for debate, political activism, protest and solidarity. With the changes in Turkish society and Turkish political scene, various protest scenes and movement are taking root in Turkey. This creates a huge clash and contrast with the ruling class and general public as Turkish culture and public traditionally accepted as requiring a leading figure to be followed with utmost authority. Talking about "unwanted" or "marginal" groups as LGBTI community this clash is even more evident and strong.
This strong clash and visible rejection from the ruling class makes LGBTI Pride Week and March as a leading and rather vivacious event in Turkish protesting movement. With the evolution of Pride Week and Pride March recently both activities are heavily associated with arts. This creates an incubator for protests art and performance.
In this paper aims at investigating the evolution of incubator status of Pride Week and March, the collective importance of both activities as protest art and finally how it creates a collaborative performance by using means such as activism, dance, music, political opposition and of course art itself.
