TI  - Digital Design and Its Socio-Political Conditions: Visual Diversity in Urbanscapes
AU  - Morales, W
T2  - PERSPECTIVES ON DESIGN AND DIGITAL COMMUNICATION: RESEARCH, INNOVATIONS AND BEST PRACTICES
A2  - Martins, N
A2  - Brandao, D
A2  - Raposo, D
AB  - The aim of this paper is to point out the theoretical importance of calling into question the socio-political sources of digital design. Precisely, by means of that idea of "sources of fiction" partly imported from the philosophy of literature and art, I postulate a distinction between digital design and visual world akin to that distinction between world fiction and natural and social world. If we comprehend the digital design in that way, many socio-political aspects of our everyday visual world are as important as the specific technical, practical and theoretical characteristics of digital design. In order to achieve that aim, I introduce and analyze the special aestheticization of the urbanscape of Montevideo City (Uruguay). The general thesis of this paper is that a really democratic digital design is also that design whose more basic visual materials are socially heterogeneous, because the democracy is not only a set of agreements among the members of the whole social body, but also the public acknowledgement of disagreements.
