TI  - The Utility Component in Two-Stage Explanations of Criminal Action Theoretical and statistical modelling of the perception-choice process
AU  - Eifler, S
AU  - Leitgob, H
T2  - MONATSSCHRIFT FUR KRIMINOLOGIE UND STRAFRECHTSREFORM
AB  - Based on a wide version of rational choice theory, the paper aims at explaining and empirically analysing criminal action in a situation-specific manner as the result of a two-stage decision process. Theoretically, we draw special attention to the relevance of utility components for criminal decision making, while the empirical focus is on the formulation of an adequate statistical model for two-stage sequential decision processes. Empirical analyses are based on data collected by means of a mail survey (n = 2,383) of a disproportionally stratified random sample of residents from an East German city. For hypothesis testing, we estimate a series of sequential logit-models from the general class of discrete choice models (Hensher et al. 2005; Train 2009). Results indicate that aspects of utility guide actions on both stages of the decision process. Finally, these implications are being discussed from a theoretical and methodological perspective.
