TY  - JOUR
TI  - Assessing and Treating Traumatic Stress in Crime Victims
AU  - Leclerc, Marie-Eve
AU  - Delisle, Catherine
AU  - Wemmers, Jo-Anne
AU  - Brunet, Alain
AB  - In a given year, one in seven victims of violent crime experiences trauma-related symptoms congruent with post-traumatic stress disorders following the victimization. Early detection and assessment of traumatic stress exposure in victims of crime are essential in order to provide the appropriate referral, and consequently reducing risks that victims develop a trauma-related disorder. Summary This project was funded by the Victims Fund of the Department of Justice Canada. In order to adequately serve and support victims of crime, it is important to understand the psychological changes that result from victimization. This Research Brief reviews the literature pertaining to traumatic stress experienced in the context of criminal victimization. It is designed as a reference tool for those who deliver services and support these victims. This document is divided into three sections. (1) The first section presents a number of the key concepts. (2) The second section reviews knowledge on crisis intervention, and offers guidelines to prepare the assessment interview. (3) The third section offers tools for the assessment of traumatic stress, as well as guidelines to orient crime victims to the appropriate treatment modality.
