TY  - JOUR
TI  - Art Galleries, auction houses and urban art: evolution and parallelisms in independent art practices
AU  - Garcia, R L A
T2  - GE-CONSERVACION
AB  - With the expansion of independent public art practices and their recognition as accessible and acceptable to the public, their forms were introduced and expanded into traditional environments such as galleries and auction houses. Consequently, specialists, gallerists and auctioneers recognised the commercial potential of these works, and accepted graffiti and street art within the contemporary art market repertoire. This originally independent art currently found in the private sector is what is known internationally as "Urban Art".
Historically, the birth, evolution and crossing of borders of practices from public spaces to their inclusion in galleries are relatively recent events. However, much has occurred from the first exhibitions of the 1970s to the present day. This article will look at everything that has happened since then, showing how the inclusion of works by graffiti writers and street artists in the commercial art market has influenced the art and its conservation.
