AU  - Banjanin, M
T2  - KNJIZEVNA SMOTRA
AB  - In his seminal book The Country and the City Raymond Williams argues convincingly that the fragmentary city scene in literary works can be viewed "as a perceptual condition" that is related to other forms of modern imagery evident in painting and in film. He notes particularly direct relations with the motion picture, "especially in its development in cutting and montage, and the characteristic movement of an observer in the close and miscellaneous environment of the streets" (242).
I focused on how the poetic form arises from the sequences of walking and seeing in the city. The poet's wanderings within the city determine the visual and spatial characteristic of urban images within the poems. I considered how the poses of Charles Baudelaire's flaneur are transmuted in the work of Aleksandr Blok and Elena Guro into moving, cinematic frames that alternately reflect and generate a new way of being in the world. We noted how the broken fragments, fragments of political and social life in the city streets (traffic, street lights, signboards, stores, buildings, crowds), fragments of inner lives of the people on the streets (artists, poets, teachers, prostitutes, snatches of conversation), and the rapid movement between them, help form a montage of the city. Sentences broken off in midair, ellipses, periods, commas, and question marks are characteristic of such prose and poetry and they anticipate Sergei Eisenstein's concept of montage by a few years (Berman 256).
