AU  - Serra, I
T2  - FORUM FOR MODERN LANGUAGE STUDIES
AB  - This essay considers a specific transformation of Italy's geographical and spiritual landscape caused by mass migration. Geographically, the large number of migrant departures left a legacy of six thousand ghost towns, crumbling and silent, full of the sentiment of 'what could have been and is no more'. More than towns, these places are metaphors. Spiritually, the children of displaced parents inherited a crumbling homeland of the mind as a blurry origin. This research starts from a rhetorical analysis of abandoned places - Italian villages and the Ellis Island hospital - and, in parallel, considers poems by representative Italian-American poets such as John Ciardi, Sandra Gilbert, Daniela Gioseffi, Paul Mariani, Dana Gioia and Felix Stefanile. This exploration culminates in the reading of the epic poem Ellis Island by Robert Viscusi and alongside the work by contemporary graffiti artist J.R. on the run-down walls of Ellis Island.
