TI  - Social Disorganization and Opportunity Structures Differential effects of social and economic conditions on crime rates related to places of offense and places of residence in the municipalities of Baden-Wuerttemberg
AU  - Gerstner, D
AU  - Oberwittler, D
T2  - MONATSSCHRIFT FUR KRIMINOLOGIE UND STRAFRECHTSREFORM
AB  - This paper examines differences in the crime load in the German region of Baden-Wuerttemberg on the low spatial level of municipalities (N = 1.109). Using multivariate regression models, the effects of social and economic conditions on violent and property crimes are analyzed and referenced to the theoretical approaches of social disorganization and routine activities. While both theories prove to be of importance, their relative strength depends on the perspective either on the criminality of the resident population or on the attractiveness of the municipalities as crime targets and, in relation to this, on the spatial mobility of offenders from their places of residence to the places of offense. Finally, it can be shown that variables assigned to social disorganization and routine activities theories interact with each other.
