TY  - JOUR
TI  - WOMENS NON-SPOUSAL MULTIPLE VICTIMIZATION - A TEST OF THE ROUTINE ACTIVITIES THEORY
AU  - RODGERS, K
AU  - ROBERTS, G
T2  - CANADIAN JOURNAL OF CRIMINOLOGY-REVUE CANADIENNE DE CRIMINOLOGIE
AB  - This analysis examines the applicability of the ''routine activities'' theory to women's risk of non-spousal multiple victimization.  Routine activities/lifestyle/exposure/opportunity theories have typically been used to explain public violence, that is, incidents of crime that occur in the public domain usually by men against men.  Rarely have these theories been tested specifically against women's victimization.  Using data collected through Statistics Canada's national Violence Against Women Survey (VAWS), both bivariate and multivariate analyses are used to examine the impact of lifestyle, proximity, exposure to crime, and physical and social guardianship on women's multiple non-spousal victimization.  In addition, because a relatively high incidence of crimes committed against women are perpetrated by someone known to them, this analysis compares stranger multiple victimization and multiple victimizations by known men in order to delineate differences in the applicability of the theory.  Results from this study showed limited support for this theoretical approach.  Proximity, exposure, and guardianship variables used in the model has none or only a minimal amount of explanatory power, and the impact of demographic measures on multiple victimization rates were not appreciably diminished by controlling for these other measures.  The study concludes by arguing that there is a need to reconcile theories of criminality and of victimization, and to incorporate theories that focus on the broader social context in which women's victimization occurs.
