TI  - Deviant Behavior as a Rational Choice
AU  - Gautschi, T
AU  - Berger, R
T2  - MONATSSCHRIFT FUR KRIMINOLOGIE UND STRAFRECHTSREFORM
AB  - The conception of man as a rational actor who considers expected benefits and costs for decision-making and consequently chooses the best alternative is an integral part of social and economic theories. For quite some time now, this originally economic approach has focused on socio-theoretical questions that are approached in interdisciplinary cooperation. The goal of these efforts is nothing more nor less than to present an integrative theory of human behavior that has explanatory power beyond economic phenomena. Although proponents of almost all "hyphenated sociologies" accuse economics and its rational choice approach of neglecting important structural factors, social norms, or institutional influences, this critique primarily aims at the conception of homo oeconomicus implied in neoclassical economics. However, there is not the rational choice theory. Nowadays, a variety of explanatory approaches are summarized under the generic term of the economic program. Against this background, this paper informs on variants, central assumptions, and predictions of theories of rational choice and their empirical evidence. On the other hand, it discusses a rational choice approach to criminal behavior and its implications. For several decades substantial contributions have been published in this field and knowledge has been accumulated on this topic.
