TI  - The Reciprocal Relationship Between Gang Involvement and Victimization by Peers: Findings from the Pittsburgh Girls Study
AU  - Gilman, AB
AU  - Howell, JC
AU  - Hipwell, AE
AU  - Stepp, SD
T2  - JOURNAL OF DEVELOPMENTAL AND LIFE-COURSE CRIMINOLOGY
AB  - Purpose While research has consistently shown a correlation between gang involvement and peer victimization, less is known about the direction of this relationship, especially among girls. The current study tests competing hypotheses to determine whether girls become gang involved following victimization, whether they experience peer victimization as result of gang involvement or whether reciprocal relationships exist.
Methods Data were drawn from the Pittsburgh Girls Study, a longitudinal panel study of 2450 girls oversampled from low-income urban neighborhoods. Gang involvement and peer victimization were assessed annually using self-report between ages 11 and 15.
Results Prevalence of gang involvement between 11 and 15 years was 6.9 %. The results of a cross-lagged model showed that there was a reciprocal relationship between gang involvement and peer victimization. Specifically, there were significant paths from gang involvement at ages 11-12 to peer victimization at age 13, from peer victimization at age 13 to gang involvement at age 14, and finally, from gang involvement at age 14 to peer victimization at age 15.
Conclusions The current study suggests that, for girls, gang involvement and peer victimization are significantly correlated across adolescence and that the relationship between these two events is reciprocal. We discuss possible theoretical explanations for these results, including the "self-help" perspective as well as the lifestyle-routine activities perspective.
