TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Theory of Rational Action as a Foundation of an Analytical Criminology
AU  - Opp, KD
AU  - Pauwels, L
T2  - MONATSSCHRIFT FUR KRIMINOLOGIE UND STRAFRECHTSREFORM
AB  - We propose a research program that we call "Analytical Criminology." An important component of this program is a wide version of rational choice theory (RCT) that is increasingly applied in the social sciences. This is the foundation for the explanation of social processes ("mechanisms") on the micro as well as on the macro level. Another goal of our program is the systematic empirical comparison of RCT with other criminological theories and the stepwise integration of the theories. It goes without saying that the formulation of precise theories is a requirement for enabling a comparison of theories. Another goal of our research program is to formulate theories with a high informative content. This means, among other things, that the theories are capable of explaining specific kinds of criminal action. In what follows this research program is presented in detail.
The situational action theory (SAT) is considered by many criminologists to be one of the most promising criminological theories. Advocates of SAT reject RCT. It seemed therefore useful to compare both theories. It is shown that proponents of SAT criticize only a narrow version of RCT. Our detailed analysis of both theories showed that SAT has some unresolved issues and is thus not a superior alternative to RCT.
