TI  - A Life-Course Theory and Long-Term Project on Trajectories of Crime
AU  - Sampson, RJ
AU  - Laub, JH
T2  - MONATSSCHRIFT FUR KRIMINOLOGIE UND STRAFRECHTSREFORM
AB  - In this article we contest the idea that individual characteristics and experiences in childhood are sufficient to explain trajectories of adult criminality, whether theoretically or empirically. We propose instead an age-graded theory designed to account for stability and change in crime over the life course from childhood to old age. This theory has been elaborated, tested, and revised based on a long-term longitudinal project which we inherited and then expanded. Our fundamental argument is that both persistent offending and desistance can be meaningfully understood within the same theoretical framework, namely, a revised age-graded theory of informal social control. The theory is reviewed alongside findings from longitudinal data on crime from childhood to age 70 that permit a prospective engagement with debates on age and crime, mechanisms of desistance, and offender-based typologies that assume distinct etiologies of crime for different groups of offenders. We also stress the overlooked importance of human agency in the development of crime and its continuation or cessation. Overall we envision development as the constant interaction between individuals and social context, coupled with random developmental noise and a purposeful human agency that is nonetheless distinct from rational choice. Contrary to most developmental theories in criminology, we view crime as an emergent process reducible neither to the individual nor the environment.
