TI  - A CHRONOLOGY OF MAJOR EVENTS RELATING TO THE CENTRAL KALAHARI GAME RESERVE II: AN UPDATE.
AU  - Hitchcock, Robert K.
AU  - Vinding, Diana
T2  - Botswana Notes & Records
AB  - The British administration of the Bechuanaland Protectorate in 1961 proclaimed the existence of the vast Central Kalahari Game Reserve. Its successor, the government of independent Botswana, promised in 1996 not to resettle the population of the game reserve, whose former hunting and foraging subsistence came to depend increasingly on goods and services provided by three local district councils and Gaborone. During the middle of 1997, three-fourths of this population was resettled by the government outside the game reserve in two settlements, which lacked firewood and adequate wild plant and water resources. The organization First People of the Kalahari (FPK) attempted to negotiate with Gaborone, but the Botswana government arrested a number of Kalahari people for "illegal hunting." In 2001, it refused to renew their game licenses, and the Ghanzi District Council stopped providing services to them. By 2002, fewer than seventy adult Kalahari people remained in the game reserve, the relocated population lived in impoverished tent encampments, and the High Court of Botswana had dismissed the case brought against the government by the FPK.
