TY  - JOUR
TI  - Raising Sioma
AU  - Cunliffe, S.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Points out that Zambia's third-largest national park, the Sioma Ngwezi National Park is located between the Zambezi and Kwando rivers and an almost unknown wilderness area. Emphasises however that since its inclusion into the Kavango-Zambezi Transfrontier Conservation Area (KAZA TFCA) Sioma Ngwezi has enormous conservation and tourism potentials. Explains that generous funding from donors and guidance of the Peace Parks Foundation, newly established concessions and camps could assist the park to rid itself of the negative reputation of being a poacher's haven. States that although the park is an integral link in the migratory route of wildlife, its resources and wildlife have however been illegally harvested for decades by soldiers during the Namibian liberation struggle, followed by refugees from Angola and finally the Lozi people turning to it for their livelihoods. Mentions rampant poaching was just one of the problems facing Sioma Ngwezi, as human settlements and land-use sprung up, including villages, livestock, cultivated fields and illegal timber-harvesting operations. Refers to the 2008 plans to expand management and development of the park, protecting the wildlife, new access roads and game -viewing tracks. Includes confessions of former poachers who turned guides and travel information of Sioma Ngwezi
