TY  - JOUR
TI  - Gardeners of Eden
AU  - Blake, S.
AU  - Africa Geographic
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Discusses the role that elephants play in the equatorial jungles of Africa, especially those in the Congo Basin of Central Africa. Points out that these forest elephants have largely eluded the great ivory plunder of the colonial times and even the 1970s and 1980s. States these small forest dwellers are the natural engineers shaping and regenerating the African rainforests, since many species of plants rely on forest elephants to disseminate their seeds. Relates that in ancient times Africa contained several million elephants, larger savanna elephants occupying the open habitats, while forest elephants were confined to the tropical jungles of West and Central Africa. States however that waves of ivory-hungry traders and hunters of Egypt, Greece and Rome began pushing savanna elephants out of their home range along the Mediterranean coast. Looks at the plunder of West African elephants to satisfy the ivory demands in Europe and America, following with extermination of southern African and East African populations. Warns that despite the current network of national parks in the Congo Basin, these last protected areas of forest elephants are also under threat from oil exploitation, mining, logging and illegal poaching and trading of ivory and elephant meat
