TI  - POACHING AS A SYMBOLIC SUBSTITUTE FOR WAR IN TUDOR AND EARLY STUART ENGLAND.
AU  - Manning, Roger B.
T2  - Journal of Medieval & Renaissance Studies
AB  - Poaching symbolically simulated the rituals and elements of traditional land warfare. For rival factions of the gentry and peerage, elements such as taunts, challenges, wearing of armor, display of trophies, and martial posturing gave their illegal hunting the air of battle. Despite injury and occasional death to gamekeepers, poaching was not severely punished and displayed a mock-heroic sensibility for the English warrior aristocracy.
