TY  - JOUR
TI  - In the line of fire: around Africa
AU  - Cunliffe, S.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Reflects on the dangers encountered by wildlife officers during their daily fight against the poaching of game. Emphasises that although many people talk about protecting wildlife and some are directly involved from the safety of an office, it is the law enforcement officers directly in the line of fire who risks their lives in confronting illegal hunters. Looks at the murdering of a wildlife officer stationed at the Phwadzi Camp in Malawi's Majete Wildlife Reserve in October 1995. Explains that he and his two colleagues came across poachers who were heavily armed, but they had to be arrested by the lightly armed officers. States wounded Lennox Mabocas, was later found executed by the poachers. Points out that his friend, Tizola Moyo, dedicated his life to the protection of wild animals of Majete Wildlife Reserve, becoming second-in-command of Majete's law enforcement division. Relates that in response to other Malawian wildlife officers killed due to lack of firepower and weapons training, the United States government provided these skills and M16 automatic rifles to fight the poachers. Mentions the African Parks Network arrived on the scene to manage Majete in 2003 and declared an amnesty period to collect poaching implements from nearby villagers
