TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hunting the wrong kind of animal in Luangwa Valley, Zambia
AU  - Conradie, G.
T2  - Wild & jag = Game & hunt
AB  - Relates the fighting of poaching in the Luangwa National Parks of Zambia. Points out that the author was tasked with the management of the Luangwa Wilderness Lodge, the only lodge in the Park, and one of the tasks was to undertake anti-poaching activities. States as he was still very young and came from the Stellenbosch winelands, this was all new and exciting. Mentions on a cold night in August 2001 he and the local staff members heard shots from the Chipuka Plains and both the author and the government anti-poaching scout realised it was too dangerous to aproach these illegal hunters at night. Narrates the events that followed, namely driving to the head office of the anti-poaching unit at Janjusi, assistance from the local tribal chief to acquire scouts to locate the poachers, the stalking of the poachers through dense mopane thickets and the eventual apprehending of the criminals. States that the poachers later each received a six-year prison sentence in the regional court. Illustrates with colour photographs, explaining how the poachers kill wild animals, such as elephants and hippos in the game reserve for their meat and poison vultures for muti, or traditional medicine. Provides contact details of the author for more information or support
