AU  - Hanes, S.
AU  - Whittam, L.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Comments on the translocation of six elephants from South Africa to the Gorongosa National Park in Mozambique during September 2008. States the elephants were a donation of SANParks to relocate the big-tusked elephants in a reserve that was once a top tourist destination, before civil war tore the country apart after the 1975 independence. Mentions that the elephants were slaughtered for food and ivory, therefore only 300 remained that were mostly tuskless. Points out that one of the six elephants translocated was a seventonner of more than forty years old, ideal to diversify the local gene pool. Relates however that he escaped from the park after only two weeks, possibly because of bush fires from poachers and had to be immobilised for the journey back after being recaptured. Regrets that he died on the road back and was buried after his tusks were removed to deter ivory seekers. Looks at an incident that ocurred in the Nxai Pans area of Botswana when the author was on an early morning drive. Explains he stopped his vehicle to listen for alarm calls alerting him to predators active at their kill. Describes lions feeding on a kudu carcass with a group of black-backed jackals in attendance, taking the gaps to scavenge on the scraps in a definite pecking order
