TY  - JOUR
TI  - Shooting grey-wing francolin in the good old days
AU  - Vary, C.
T2  - Wild & jag = Game & hunt
AB  - Looks at the history of greywing francolin shooting in South Africa. Mentions in the sixteenth century the European aristocracy started using muzzle loading matchlock shotguns for shooting gamebirds and by 1680 flint and wheel-lock shotguns and shooting gamebirds on the wing became popular. States the design of guns moved from muzzle to breech loading and wingshooting were the number one recreation of the European upper class. Points out that in the 18th and 19th centuries European and British settlers arrived at the Cape Colony, bringing their rifles and shotguns. Emphasises of the 72 gamebird species found in South Africa, the wingshooters overall choice were the greywing francolin or `partridge' as it was then called. Relates the author's own experiences of shooting greywing over the years, especially in the Dordrecht district. Mentions how the enormous greywing populations were thinned out by the long shooting seasons, no bag limits and the commercial bird shoots. Comments on how Cape Nature Conservation stepped in and imposed the necessary restrictions, including a bag limit of six birds and a short season of just over two months. Advises landowners to ensure that all shooters strictly adhere to the limitations
