TI  - The edge of Eden
AU  - Cunliffe, S.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Focuses on Malawi's Liwonde National Park, that despite being only 500 square kilometres in size, is a national treasure with lush vegetation and a diverse wildlife. States the author went to investigate what was done to protect this wilderness area against the demands of the ever growing populations of the impoverished surrounding communities. Mentions the first European to set foot in this area in 1859 was David Livingstone, resting here during his travels. Explains that Liwonde was proclaimed a national park in 1973 to safeguard its biodiversity, but mismanagement, poaching and habitat encroachment prompted the Malawian government to seek help to rectify the situation. Points out assistance of South African and German conservationists in the 1990's helped to upgrade the infrastructure and anti-poaching programme. Looks at the reintroduction of various game species that made Liwonde a crucial reservoir for rare species. Reflects on the improvement of the illegal hunting situation, especially with the arrival of Wilderness Safaris in 1995, supplying employment to poachers. Warns however that as fish stocks shrink in Lake Malombe, fishermen took to illegal fishing as the rural population relies on subsistence agriculture and fishing to survive
