TY  - JOUR
TI  - Naturalist is wrong to attack Malta over bird hunt.
T2  - Daily Mail
AB  - I AM a frequent traveller to Malta, and this year took up an invitation to visit during the spring hunting season. I accepted the invitation with trepidation, having read of atrocities attributed to Malta's hunters. This short period of 19 half-days, permits the hunting of 11,000 turtle doves and 5,000 quail, and is a result of a European Court of Justice ruling that allowed a derogation from the Wild Birds Directive due to the small numbers of birds shot in autumn. I spent ten mornings with the friend who had invited me, and this taught me a lot about alleged Maltese hunting 'illegalities'. I was pleasantly surprised to note that apart from the numerous sparrows and warblers, other birds such as raptors, golden orioles, bee eaters and above all the two quarry species are only present on particular days where the weather conditions are right. I did not witness any illegal hunting. Flocks of bee eaters and golden orioles would pass over hunters, and harriers on the hunt often stopped within feet of the hides, yet not once did I see anyone disturb these birds. All that was shot — and not that frequently — were turtle doves and the odd quail flushed by the dogs. [ABSTRACT FROM PUBLISHER]
