TY  - JOUR
TI  - The price of protection: around Africa
AU  - Cunliffe, S.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Discusses some of the challenges facing the game rangers trying to protect the threatened wildlife species of the Garamba National Park in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC). Points out that this park lies in a couldron of conflict where poachers and a violent rebel group, the Lord's Resistance Army (LRA) were plundering the wild animals. Looks at the military-style training for the rangers in their battle against the pillagers. Explains that a former officer in Kenya Wildlife Service is in charge of this training of rangers employed by the Institut Congolais pour la Conservation de la Nature (ICCN), endeavouring to re-establish control of the park. Emphasises that the Kenyan Paul Onyango is the ideal candidate to rein the poachers at Garamba, while turning this disparate crew of the ICCN into a highly effective anti-poaching unit. States as the volatile security situation in north-eastern DRC appears to be stabilising, poaching in Garamba is being curbed and an expanded ranger force is wresting back control of the park. Mentions the biggest problems currently are the Congolese soldiers who eat everything from monkeys to elephants, but there is still hope that the wildlife can be saved. Provides the Internet address of Garamba for more information
