TI  - Useful information from BirdLife South Africa (from SABirdNet)
AU  - Verdoorn, G
T2  - Diaz Diary
AB  - AFRICAN PUBLICATIONS Abstract: "It is an offence to discharge a firearm, an antique firearm or an airgun in a built up area or any public place, without good reason to do so." Shooting a bird of whatever species will not be constituted as "good reason" even if the bird is an alien invader. BirdLife South Africa therefore advises the public that in terms of the FCA it is a criminal offence to shoot any bird in a public place or built-up area with an air rifle even if it is a common mynah. We also advise the public that it is an offense to shoot indigenous birds in public or other places with air guns. Air guns are declared as "illegal hunting methods" in conservation ordinances and may thus not be used for hunting. Only certain bird species like game birds and waterfowl may be legally hunted with shot guns and centre fire calibres AND with a hunting license that is issued for particular species. Even land owners of exemption farms need hunting licenses for the hunting of game birds and waterfowl. In some provinces that have yet to revise their ordinances it may still be possible to hunt non-game birds without a hunting license but the hunter still requires a written authorisation from the land owner. This should change when new legislation and ordinances are promulgated. There are some cases where the magistrate of a town may grant people authorisation to shoot mynahs under the strict supervision of the SA Police Service. I have lodged an enquiry with the Registrar of Firearms about the use of air guns in towns and cities as many people complain that the SA Police Service refuses to proceed with charges and to investigate when such misdemeanor is reported based on the fact that air guns are not classified as firearms any longer. BirdLife SA has had no response from the Registrar about this. I suggest that all birders take note of the subsection 7 as stated above and insist that the police officer takes a statement and lays a charge against the culprit. Should this fail, I suggest that you take the name and service number of the officer and report that to me. I will compile a report and submit this to the Registrar of Firearms via the Hunters' Forum that meets with the Registrar on a regular basis. Air guns are not toys and should not be handled by people younger than 18 years of age unless supervised by adults. As Vice-President Conservation, of the SA Hunters and Game Conservation I can state that we are strictly opposed to: the use of air guns by minors unless supervised by responsible adults, the use of air guns in public or built up places and the use of air guns for any hunting
