TY  - JOUR
TI  - Around Africa: Forgotten antelope
AU  - Rovero, F.
AU  - Davenport, T.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Reports on the authors' efforts to find and protect the rare Abbott's duiker. Explains these forest-dwelling antelopes are highly specialised for living in moist, evergreen forests, as duikers have short stocky limbs developed to crawl through tangled vegetation. States they are mostly small to medium-sized, while Abbott's duiker can reach 60 kilogrammes. Mentions this duiker is known as `minde' and endemic to just a few isolated sites in Tanzania, in five mountain ranges, including the Southern Highlands and the Eastern Arc Mountains, with Udzungwa Mountains supporting the largest population. Warns Abbott's duiker is extremely rare and highly threatened, following decades of illegal hunting. States researchers and biologists such as the authors find it difficult to reach and study these animals, because of their preference to remote montane forests and extreme shyness, predominantly nocturnal habits and low population density. Points out therefore their real conservation status in view to ensure their survival is challenging. Mentions this conservation project was taken to the national level, with collaboration of The Wildlife Conservation Society and Tanzania Wildlife Research Institute in order to understand their optimal habitat, genetic diversity and threats to them
