TY  - JOUR
TI  - National Oribi Survey 2007 - Positive results
AU  - Evans, S.W.
T2  - Wild & jag = Game & hunt
AB  - Points out that the response to the national oribi census of 2007 was very positive, with conscious effort made to count these antelopes throughout their range. States the Endangered Wildlife Trust (EWT) Oribi Working Group (OWG) received 185 returns with a combined total of 993 oribi (Ourebia ourebi) counted in 2007, the majority from KwaZulu-Natal and Mpumalanga with the largest oribi populations. Warns of alarming statistics indicating that oribi numbers in South Africa are still decreasing in comparison with figures from previous years. Emphasises according to landowners who participated in the survey, the primary reason for the continued decrease in oribi numbers is illegal hunting such as poaching, also habitat destruction. Explains that the national bi-annual oribi count serves to determine trends in the oribi population, thus assisting in future conservation management decisions regarding this endangered species, determining the current primary threats to oribi and involving landowners in the conservation activities of the EWT-OWG, including conservation of oribi. Provides contact details for more information
