TY  - CHAP
TI  - Politicians and poachers: the political economy of wildlife policy in Afrika
AU  - Gibson, Clark C.
AB  - NIZA Abstract: Clark Gibson analyses the politics of wildlife policy in Zambia, Kenya, and Zimbabwe. The history of these policies is disappointing. Nationalist governments failed to keep their pre-independent promises to elimiinate colonial wildlife laws. Bureaucracies created policies frustrating some conservation goals, while other policies designed to create animal-conserving incentives failed to stop illegal hunting. The reasons are complex. A constant theme is that individuals and groups sought to create policies advantageous to themselves.Their strategies reflected incentives created by current institutions. As institutions changed, so did strategies and policies, but not in ways necessarily conducive to conservation. Gibson analyses decisions at the local level, where most of the important ones about wildlife amnagement are actually taken
