TY  - JOUR
TI  - If it stays, it pays
AU  - Michler, I.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Focuses on claims and counter-claims with regard to the effectiveness of the hunting industry in conserving the wildlife of Africa. States that the trophy hunting associations in Africa and elsewhere have always claimed to be opposed to canned hunting, the shooting of captive-bred animals. Emphasises recent CITES statistics on export and import of lion trophies indicated however that from a total of 1172 trophy hunting permits for lions issued in 2008 and 2009, 707 were for captive-bred animals. Mentions that 406 of these were allocated to United States of America hunters, therefore still the major supporters of canned hunting. Reflects on the staggering per cent annual increase in canned hunting of lions since 1994, when records were first kept, showing that the trophy hunting industry is ineffective at policing itself. Warns of international trophy hunting, as well as illegal killings or domestic off-take in African countries of wild lions. Explains another claim of hunters, namely that canned hunting relieves hunting pressure on wild lion populations, is also refuted as statistics show wild lions are also incleasingly hunted. Looks at the 2008 assessment of the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species, indicating the steep decline of lion populations, especially of male lions
