TY  - JOUR
TI  - Learning the trade - can we always do everything right?
AU  - Rabe, F.
T2  - Africa's bowhunter & archer
AB  - Reflects on ethics, laws and practice in terms of hunting, especially bowhunting. Looks at the hunter-gatherers of ancient times who made the first crude bows to feed their clans if successful. States that there were no ethics involved, however today ethics have become part of the obstacles to overcome, since bowhunters have to face the onslaught of the anti-hunting fraternity. Askes which laws to follow that will make bowhunters worthy, as in 1990 bowhunting was still illegal in all of South Africa, because no one thought it humane or ethical to hunt with such equipment. Mentions ten years later every animal not totally protected has been hunted, even all of the Big Six, and some of the bowhunters took it upon themselves to do tests and learn from the mistakes that were made. Regrets however some people started abusing the system and hunted without proper knowledge, therefore bowhunters can no longer hunt the Big Six in South Africa. Emphasises as bowhunting equipment develops so fast to keep up with technology it must be tested to make a clean kill. Explains bows became stronger and faster, arrows changed from wood to aluminium-carbon-composite, broadheads from carbon steel to stainless steel and two-blade to four-blade mechanical
