TY  - JOUR
TI  - Still surviving
AU  - Michler, I.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Looks at the current state of Zimbabwean wildlife and wilderness areas, since the country is on the brink of collapse. Explains that the first land invasions began in late 1999 and early 2000, what turned out to be a land grab sanctioned by the government, thus destroying the once-flourishing agricultural sector. Reports that rumours persisted of widespread game annihilation, targeting wildlife conservancies mostly privately controlled by white landowners. States in spite of the mass killings of wild animals in these conservancies, the national parks and reserves were relatively unscathed due largely to the exceptional efforts of idividuals, companies and dedicated Zimbabwe parks employees. Wonders how long this state of affairs could continue given the desperate scenario of unemployment, poverty and food scarcity crises. Warns of numerous reports that the situation has worsened, because of a substantial increase in poaching and signs of syndicates targeting rhino populations, also 1000 animals licenced for trophy hunting and possible resuming of culling practices. Regrets in this turbulent times unscrupulous United Kingdom and South African hunting outfitters work with corrupt officials to kill game within national parks, even entire elephant herds
