TY  - JOUR
TI  - Ian Michler's Diary: Tiger-bone wine
AU  - Michler, I.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Investigates the murky world of trophy hunting, poaching and the rise of animal-trading syndicates. Mentions traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) has an acient heritage and is linked to Taoist and Buddhist philosophies. States that some herbal and other medicinal treatments are traditionally mixed from natural components such as plant, mineral and also animal products. Warns however that a number of the animal species used to make these medicines are now listed as threatened or endangered, since the cures and remedies are made from their body parts. Points out that as many high-profile animals, including rhinos, tigers, musk deer, bears and seahorses have become international conservation issues, the TCM acquired its somewhat tarnished reputation. Looks at tiger bone used in the making of a wine advocated to bring relief in bone-related ailments such as arthritis and rheumatism. Comments on the trade in lion bones increasing since trading in tiger body parts was banned. Recommends authorities to investigate the possibility of a criminal element in the links between rhino trophy hunting in South Africa, the increase in poaching levels of rhino, the rapid rise in the price of lion bones and the Far East based animal-trading syndicates operating in southern Africa
