TY  - JOUR
TI  - Pet a cub today, can tomorrow?
AU  - Trendler, K.
AU  - Joubert, L.
AU  - Wright, N.
T2  - Wild & jag = Game & hunt
AB  - Looks at the interaction of humans with lion cubs, young cheetah and even tigers in various facilities over South Africa. States these visitors petting the young wild animals seldom ask what will become of the cubs when they grow too big for the facilities to manage. Regrets that there is substantial evidence to suggest that these animals are often sold or `returned' to predator breeding facilities, ending up as trophies into the very lucrative canned hunting industry. Explains that cubs are taken from their mothers to stimulate faster reproduction and to keep up the supply of petting lions. Warns however that these lions are human imprinted and have not grown up in a natural social group, thus making it impossible to release them into a natural habitat for the long term. Mentions some questions that can be asked by visitors before playing with cubs, such as where is the cub's mother and why is the cub not raised by her, also what happens to the cubs when they grow up. Points out that captive breeding is not a conservation recommendation, since carnivores breed extremely well in the right conditions, and for almost all of our threatened carnivore species the conservation priorities include securing suitable habitat and reducing poaching. Provides contact details
