TY  - JOUR
TI  - Talking of nature: signing off
AU  - Hanks, J.
T2  - Africa Geographic
AB  - Points out that the dugong is a fascinating species, but since it has not received the necessary attention, it has become one of Africa's most endangered species. States the author does not understand how the dugong could be responsible for the myth of the mermaid, especially as seen up close the aquatic mammal of 300 kilogramme does not resemble a woman with a tail fin. Explains that dugong, also known as `sea cows' and `sea pigs', is a member of the order Sirenia. Mentions it has evolved from the same ancestors as the elephant, with which it shares a number of features, such as also being herbivorous, grazing on seagrass with its uniquely shaped snout and both species live for about sixty years. Warns that the dugong is rapidly declining or even becoming extinct, because it lost at least a third of its global range of estimated 140 000 kilometres, as noted by the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species. States dugongs are under threat due to a combination of activities, including destruction of their seagrass beds by pollution, illegal harvesting and deaths in gill nets. Mentions also disturbances from noise of motorised boat traffic and tourism growth. Looks at marine protected areas such as Bazaruto National Park in Mozambique with a potential to be a dugong sanctuary
