TY  - JOUR
TI  - Free-roaming kudu of the Northern Cape: an endangered heritage?
AU  - Anon
T2  - Wild & jag = Game & hunt
AB  - Warns that the free-roaming kudu of the Northern Cape could vanish as part of our natural heritage as the result of factors such as intensive irrigation and stock farming, illegal hunting and poaching. States the triangle formed by the towns of Douglas, Griekwastad and Niekerkshoop forms the heart of the kudu habitat. Points out that the settlement of San and Tshwana people in this area proved fatal for the resident kudu population as livestock farming and drought threatened the ecosystem. Emphasises because of the four months hunting season and basically non-existent controlling of hunting activities, due to the shortage of Nature Conservation and SAPS officials, any number of kudu could be shot. Reports this study has shown that conservation efforts regarding kudu by some landowners were poor as kudu are being shot indiscriminately and for commercial meat purposes. Looks at some suggestions by concerned farmers of the area to tackle this problem, namely meetings with relevant authorities, access control of some roads, a conservation programme, meetings with hunting associations, schedulling of a two month hunting season and sponsorships to ensure that the project continues
