TI  - Highlights: Sub-Saharan Africa Health Report 1-15 Jan 08
DA  - 2008/01/01/
PY  - 2008
DP  - EBSCOhost
UR  - https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=awn&AN=FP20080117601002&site=ehost-live
DB  - awn
N1  - <p>Note: Highlights: Sub-Saharan Africa Health Report 1-15 Jan 08&nbsp;&nbsp; The following is a roundup of selected health reporting monitored in Sub-Saharan Africa from 1 through 15 January 2008&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; HIV/AIDS&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Southern Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Health Authorities Express Concern at Nampula Province Rising HIV/AIDS, STD Rates&nbsp;&nbsp; Wamphula Fax reported on 4 January that sexually transmitted diseases, STDs, are on the rise in Nampula Province, a situation that is worrying the health authorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;In 2007, 47,485 cases of STDs and HIV/AIDS were reported, compared to 7,901 in 2006.&nbsp;&nbsp;The reasons include the lack of awareness, the fact that most anti-HIV projects are concentrated in the City of Nampula, the fact that subsidies for HIV-positive public servants have not been paid, and the relegating of community and religious leaders to the bottom of programs preventing STDs and HIV/AIDS. [Nampula Wamphula Fax (Internet Version-E-mail) in Portuguese -- Privately owned newssheet published twice weekly, focusing primarily on news from Nampula Province]&nbsp;&nbsp; Swaziland: AIDS Support Center Begins Psycho-Social Counseling&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On 8 January, The Times of Swaziland reported that: "the AIDS Support Center has reinforced its HIV/AIDS unit by venturing into psycho-social counseling as part of the HIV/AIDS pre-test and post-test counseling package.&nbsp;&nbsp;This unit has placed its emphasis on the fact that HIV is not an isolated issue in a person's life, even more so because people need the psychological strength to deal with all the trials and tribulations of HIV/AIDS first.&nbsp;&nbsp;"The most common types of problems presented by clients during the psycho-social counseling sessions are the difficulty coping with a positive result, dealing with the AIDS-related death of loved one, feelings of guilt over infecting a child (mother-to-child transmission), relationship problems caused by one or both partners being HIV positive, and dealing with being infected by a partner." [Mbabane The Times of Swaziland (Internet Version WWW) in English -- privately owned liberal daily newspaper often critical of government policies. Internet: http://www.times.co.sz ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Angola: NGOs Warn Children in Benguela Leaving School To Look After Parents With HIV/AIDS&nbsp;&nbsp; Lusa reported on 8 January that AIDS is becoming one of the main reasons why children are dropping out of school in Benguela, according to two NGOs working in the region.&nbsp;&nbsp;Children have to look after parents with HIV/AIDS and take them to treatment centers, and these are "important reasons" why students are leaving school.&nbsp;&nbsp;Gabriel Barros, who works for the British NGO Oxfam, stated that the living conditions of AIDS patients must be improved by including them in income-producing programs.&nbsp;&nbsp;Only in this manner will they be able to keep their children at school.&nbsp;&nbsp;Children, especially girls, are "forced to stay home because their parents have died or are seriously incapacitated by the disease," he added.&nbsp;&nbsp;NGO Human Action Coordinator Pombal Maria stated that there are over 120,000 individuals who need anti-retroviral treatment in the country, but only 10,000 were supported by the government in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;He warned that many children also are discriminated against after their family members are spotted at these treatment centers, and this is another reason for dropping out of school.&nbsp;&nbsp;Many parents stop treatment to ensure that their children continue to attend school.&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, the EU has made about 3 million euros available to support local organizations in the fight against AIDS in Benguela, Huambo, Bie, and Moxico. [Lisbon Lusa News Agency WWW-Text in Portuguese -- government-owned news agency]&nbsp;&nbsp; Lesotho: AIDS Support Group Official States HIV/AIDS Testing Key to Treatment, Care, Support&nbsp;&nbsp; On 13 January, LENA reported that Mrs. Mamotlomelo Motlomelo, the chairperson of the Kutloanong Support Group, stated that HIV/AIDS testing is the key to treatment, care, and support.&nbsp;&nbsp;Mrs. Motlomelo made this remark during a one-day meeting held for people living with HIV/AIDS and their caregivers at Ha Abia.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Kutloanong Support Group is a group of 10 members that was established by some Abia villagers in 2007 with the aim to care and support HIV/AIDS patients around that area. [Maseru Lesotho News Agency E-mail-Text in English -- Quasi-government news agency]&nbsp;&nbsp; Lesotho: Health Ministry To Hold Training Workshop on PMTCT&nbsp;&nbsp; LENA reported on 14 January that the Family Health Division of the Ministry of Health and Social Welfare is to hold a one-day training workshop on the prevention of mother-to-child transmission, PMTCT, and a new method of registering expectant mothers at Mafeteng Golden Hotel on 15 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Mafeteng Government Hospital matron stated that the workshop is aimed at equipping nursing staff with knowledge and skills concerning the implementation of PMTCT in their workplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;She added that the workshop also aims to promote community education about PMTCT, in order to endorse community support to HIV-infected mothers, especially those of childbearing age.&nbsp;&nbsp; Central/West Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Benin: Doctor Praises Role of Mothers in PMTCT Efforts in Pobe District&nbsp;&nbsp; On 15 January, IRIN reported on the story of 38-year-old mother of three Rosalie Salako.&nbsp;&nbsp;Every week, she meets with pregnant women from the District of Pobe, 120 kilometers east of Cotonou, in order to encourage them to be tested for the HIV virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;Salako, who was diagnosed HIV-negative, has been involved in the prevention of mother-to-child transmission, PMTCT, for three years now.&nbsp;&nbsp;The work of Salako and other women like her in Pobe and the neighboring towns clearly is paying off, because nine out of 10 women who attend antenatal healthcare facilities in the health zone agree to be tested.&nbsp;&nbsp;She explained that her work consists of telling the women that being tested will allow them to give their children security and protect them against AIDS if they have contracted the virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;She also ensures that the HIV-positive pregnant women she advises stick to their treatment regimen, before and after the baby is born.&nbsp;&nbsp;Dr Joseph Zinsou, the PMTCT coordinating doctor in the district, stated that the role of women like Salako, who are mothers themselves, is crucial.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is because it has enabled more pregnant women to gain access to HIV/AIDS services.&nbsp;&nbsp;PMTCT is widely available in maternity hospitals in Benin, and 1,171 pregnant women tested positive for HIV in 2005.&nbsp;&nbsp;Some 939 received anti-retroviral, ARV, medication.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, it must be admitted that some attitudes hamper the tremendous job these women undertake.&nbsp;&nbsp;For example, it is hard to convince many women, some men ask their partners to not undergo the screening, and people that are known to be HIV-positive are rejected. [Nairobi UN Integrated Regional Information Network (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Non-profit, donor-supported news service of the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs; Focuses on political, economic and social issues affecting humanitarian efforts; URL as of filing date is http://www.irinnews.org/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Nigeria: Yobe State Action Committee Hands Over Funds to NGOs Combating HIV/AIDS&nbsp;&nbsp; On 4 January, NAN reported that the Yobe State Action Committee on AIDS in Damaturu is distributing 26 million naira in grants to 26 NGOs involved in the fight against HIV/AIDS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Yobe Governor Mamman Ali, who handed out the checks, stated that the grants represented 50 percent of the 52-million naira World Bank assistance to the state to enhance the fight against the scourge.&nbsp;&nbsp;On the occasion, the governor is reported to have directed that the NGOs be screened before receiving the grants.&nbsp;&nbsp;He also promised that the government will monitor their activities.&nbsp;&nbsp;Governor Ali also commended the World Bank for assisting the state in the fight against AIDS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Alhaji Dauda Yahaya, the chairman of the committee and secretary to the Yobe State Government, stated that other materials donated by the World Bank include 21 motorcycles and data management tools, as well as information and campaign materials.&nbsp;&nbsp;Alhaji Abdulazeez Mohammed, the state's monitoring and evaluation officer, stated that the 17 local government areas will be provided with a motorcycle each.&nbsp;&nbsp;He pledged that the government will monitor the use of the motorcycles and materials closely, in order to ensure that the organizations complied with the terms of agreement. [Abuja NAN (Internet Version-E-mail) in English -- e-mailed version of the state-owned, government-controlled News Agency of Nigeria]&nbsp;&nbsp; East Africa (there are no items in this category)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OTHER INFECTIOUS DISEASES&nbsp;&nbsp; Southern Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Health Official Reports Over 400 Cases of Diarrhea Registered in Harare Suburbs&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On 4 January, The Herald Online reported that more than 400 cases of diarrhea have been reported in Harare's high-density suburbs of Mabvuku and Tafara since the outbreak was detected a few weeks ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;The city's acting director of health services stated that cases of diarrhea increased over the previous month, attributing the rise to uncollected garbage, sewer blockages, and erratic water supplies.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, he could not confirm if there have been any deaths linked to the outbreak. [Harare The Herald Online (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- pro-government newspaper published daily except Sunday; Internet: http://www.herald.co.zw ]&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: Eight of 27 XDR TB Absconded Patients Returned to Port Elizabeth Hospital&nbsp;&nbsp; SAPA reported on 4 January that the Eastern Cape Health Department stated: "Eight out of the 27 XDR [extremely drug-resistant] and MDR [multidrug-resistant] tuberculosis patients who absconded from a Port Elizabeth Hospital last month were traced and returned for treatment."&nbsp;&nbsp;Spokesman Siyanda Manana added: "There still are 19 patients missing; however, we will continue with our door-to-door intervention." [Johannesburg SAPA (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Cooperative, nonprofit national news agency; Internet: http://www.sapa.org.za ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Angola: Deputy Health Minister States Cholera Outbreak Situation Improving&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On 8 January, Apostolado reported that, despite a decrease in the cholera outbreak, a total of 515 fatalities still were registered in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;During the same period, there were 18,390 cases, most of these in the Provinces of Luanda and Benguela; this amounted to 48,866 fewer cases and 2,207 fewer deaths than in 2006.&nbsp;&nbsp;The largest number of deaths, some 173, occurred in Cuanza Sul in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;Deputy Health Minister Jose Van-Dunem stated that actions undertaken by the government, such as supplying drinking water, led to these improvements.&nbsp;&nbsp;In his view, the situation is under better control because the authorities are more alert and have enough resources to deal with the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;Since the beginning of the epidemic in 2006, 85,646 cases and 3,237 deaths have been reported. [Luanda Apostolado WWW-Text in Portuguese -- Online journal sponsored by the Roman Catholic Church; Internet: http://www.apostolado.info ]&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: Limpopo Health Department Announces Malaria Outbreak Under Control&nbsp;&nbsp; On 9 January, the Sowetan reported that the Limpopo Department of Health and Social Development has downplayed fears of a malaria outbreak in the province.&nbsp;&nbsp;This follows reports that more cases of malaria have been recorded in the province than in previous years.&nbsp;&nbsp;The province has recorded 500 cases of malaria over the past two months.&nbsp;&nbsp;The provincial spokesman for the department stated that that the higher number of cases recorded meant that people were responding positively to their calls to consult in case they started feeling unwell.&nbsp;&nbsp;"In all the 500 cases that were reported over the past two months, we are proud to state that most of the patients were treated and are fine now." [Johannesburg Sowetan in English -- Privately-owned weekday regional newspaper targeting a mainstream black audience and supportive of government policies]&nbsp;&nbsp; Botswana: Health Ministry Issues Malaria Warnings for Five Areas&nbsp;&nbsp; MMEGI reported on 10 January that there were at least six malaria deaths and 16,495 malaria cases in Botswana in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Ministry of Health, this compares to 2006, where there were 42 deaths and 19,938 malaria cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;Malaria continues to be endemic in five areas of Botswana: the Okavango; Ngami; Chobe; Tutume; and Boteti.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, it has not reached epidemic levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;The ministry continues to issue warnings for people to take action and take antimalarial medicines two weeks before taking the journey to the malarial areas, as well as to continue taking it for four weeks after leaving the area.&nbsp;&nbsp;They add that people should seek medical advice before traveling to malaria-affected destinations. [Gaborone MMEGI (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Independent daily often critical of government; Internet: http://www.mmegi.bw/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: Researchers Warn XDR TB To Become Bigger Health Risk Than HIV/AIDS&nbsp;&nbsp; Pretoria News reported on 11 January said that extremely drug-resistant tuberculosis, XDR TB, could become a more serious public health risk than HIV/AIDS because of government inaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;That is the warning from a team of health researchers who assessed the risk of the usually fatal illness.&nbsp;&nbsp;They concluded that the health authorities were not doing enough to combat it.&nbsp;&nbsp;"The government's initial lethargic reaction to the crisis and uncertainty among health professionals concerning the implications of tackling this outbreak effectively highlight the urgent need to address these issues, lest inaction spawn a full-blown XDR TB epidemic," stated Jerome Amir Singh, Ross Upshur, and Nesri Padayatchi in their paper entitled "XDR TB in South Africa: No Time for Denial or Complacency." [Pretoria Pretoria News in English -- Local daily, privately owned by Independent Newspaper Company]&nbsp;&nbsp; Botswana: Reports Warns Country's TB Notification Rate One of Highest in World&nbsp;&nbsp; The Daily News Online reported on 7 January that: "the auditor general's report on the country's tuberculosis (TB) program has revealed that Botswana has one of the highest TB notification rates in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;The report adds that TB is a major public health problem in Botswana responsible for more than 10 percent of all institutional admissions and over 5 percent of outpatient department attendances.&nbsp;&nbsp;The numbers are comparable with the World Health Organization's set standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;At the time of the audit it also was discovered that there only were four full-time TB coordinators instead of 24 nationwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the report, the infrastructure was found to be inadequate for the treatment of the disease at the eight field facilities visited, thus putting patients at risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;These places were Francistown, Kasane, Selibe Phikwe, Serowe/Palapye, Masunga, Ghanzi, Kanye, and Tutume." [Gaborone Daily News Online (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- government-owned bilingual newspaper; Internet: http://www.mcst.gov.bw/dailynews/index.php ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: UNDP Report Shows 91,000 People Suffering With TB&nbsp;&nbsp; Correio da Manha reported on 10 January that close to 91,000 Mozambicans suffer from TB, which is the third-commonest cause of hospitalization.&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the United Nations Development Program Report on Human Development in 2007, the incidence of TB has been rising considerably over the past 10 years, mainly due to its association with HIV/AIDS.&nbsp;&nbsp;The report indicated that Mozambique is one of the 22 countries most-affected by the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;The rate of detection in Mozambique is around 48 percent, while the recovery rate is about 80 percent.&nbsp;&nbsp;A study conducted in Sofala by the Health Ministry found that at least 3,200 new cases of TB are registered in the region annually, a prevalence rate of 566 people out of every 100,000 inhabitants. [Maputo Correio da Manha (Internet Version-E-mail) in Portuguese -- Privately owned daily newssheet focusing primarily on economic and political issues]&nbsp;&nbsp; Angola: Health Minister Argues Fighting Cholera Priority of Health Sector&nbsp;&nbsp; On 11 January, Correio da Manha reported on an interview with Health Minister Anastacio Sicato, who stated that one of the main priorities of the health sector is fighting cholera.&nbsp;&nbsp;This involves providing people with a clean environment and drinking water.&nbsp;&nbsp;Sicato added that malaria ranks second in causing fatalities, and awareness about HIV/AIDS needs to be increased.&nbsp;&nbsp;Angola has one of the highest child mortality rates in the world; this amounts to around 120 deaths every year of children under one year of age for every 1,000 children born every year, and 260 deaths every year of children under five years of age out of every 1,000 born.&nbsp;&nbsp;Sicato added that another priority is to bring down the prenatal death rate, which is 1,000 out of every 100,000 births currently; previously it was 1,700 out of every 100,000 births.&nbsp;&nbsp;Priorities in 2008 include improving people's access to HIV/AIDS services, increasing the capacity of national hospitals, and strengthening the fight against endemic diseases, among other things.&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Provincial Health Official Argues Nampula Not in Danger of Cholera Outbreak&nbsp;&nbsp; On 11 January, Wamphula Fax reported that, judging by the reduced number of diarrhea cases being reported at clinics, Nampula may not be in danger of the annual cholera outbreak any longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;Provincial Health Director Falvio Jordao Wate stated that the province is out of danger, but five districts still are on standby.&nbsp;&nbsp;He added that there is cause for concern when the prevalence of diarrhea cases climbs to over 30 percent in adults.&nbsp;&nbsp;About two weeks earlier, 12 of the province's 20 districts were on a state of alert, including the provincial capital.&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Authorities Register 1,396 Cholera Cases, 12 Deaths Since Oct 2007&nbsp;&nbsp; Media Fax reported on 11 January that the health authorities have registered 12 cholera deaths and a total of 1,396 cholera cases from the beginning of the epidemic in October 2007 to date.&nbsp;&nbsp;The City of Maputo has been the most-affected, with 673 cases and seven deaths to date.&nbsp;&nbsp;Despite the fact that the disease is considered to be under control, the authorities remain concerned about the number of cases at the Cholera Treatment Center in the City of Maputo. [Maputo Media Fax (Internet Version-WWW) in Portuguese -- Privately owned newssheet]&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Authorities Register 26 Cholera Cases in Beira Outbreak&nbsp;&nbsp; On 12 January, Noticias reported that there has been a cholera outbreak in Beira again, and 26 cases have been registered already.&nbsp;&nbsp;The District of Chemba, which has been affected by cholera since July 2007, has been declared free of the disease, as no new cases have been reported for over 60 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;Sofala Provincial Health Official Ricardo Guilande stated that Maringue had 106 cholera cases, and the disease is under control there although the health authorities remain on alert.&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, the number of diarrhea cases increased at the Matundo Medical Center in the City of Tete and City Health Director Cecilia Matolino stated this is cause for concern, although cholera has not been identified yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;In Gaza, nine new cases have been reported and 24 patients continue to be hospitalized for cholera. [Maputo Noticias (Internet Version-WWW) in Portuguese -- Government-controlled daily newspaper; Internet: http://www.jornalnoticias.co.mz/pt/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Harare Residents Accuse Government of 'Playing Down' Diarrhea Outbreak&nbsp;&nbsp; The Standard reported on 13 January that the Combined Harare Residents Association, CHRA, has accused the government of playing down the outbreak of diarrhea in many city suburbs.&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Ministry of Health and Child Welfare, more than 400 cases have been recorded in Mabvuku and Tafara, which have been hit by an acute water shortage over the past year.&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHRA disputed this, insisting that at least 15,000 people may have been affected.&nbsp;&nbsp;The association stated that its research shows the government and the council were understating the number of victims, having counted the households affected.&nbsp;&nbsp;Some areas in Mabvuku have had no water since March.&nbsp;&nbsp;CHRA Information Officer Mfundo Mlilo stated that the situation in the twin suburbs was "very disturbing" because the Zimbabwe National Water Authority, Zinwa, and the council showed a lack of commitment to solve the crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;Mlilo added that the diarrhea outbreak was not confined to the two suburbs but also to others, where cholera and dysentery have been reported. [Harare The Standard (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Privately-owned weekly critical of government policies; Internet: http://www.thestandard.co.zw/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Government Unveils Two New Drugs To Combat Malaria&nbsp;&nbsp; According to The Herald Online on 5 January, the government introduced two new antimalarial drugs aimed at curbing the prevalence of both mosquito and malaria parasites on 4 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;The drugs coartemether, which falls under the artermisinin-based combination therapy, and bio-larvicides are expected to reduce malaria cases in Zimbabwe.&nbsp;&nbsp;Coartemether tablets are the most effective combination under ACT therapy and are known to eliminate malaria parasites from the blood of infected persons, while bio-larvicide is a concentrate applied to mosquito-breeding sites to kill mosquitoes at the larval stage.&nbsp;&nbsp;Dr. David Parirenyatwa, the minister of health and child welfare, stated that coartemether will be rolled out in most hospitals nationwide this season at no cost, while the registration of bio-larvicide was in the pipeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;"In the second year or next malaria season, the whole country is expected to be using coartemether.&nbsp;&nbsp;In areas earmarked for malaria elimination like Matabeleland South, we expect all health facilities to be using coartemether this season," stated Dr. Parirenyatwa.&nbsp;&nbsp; Central/West Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Ghana: Tema Port Health Chief Urges Inoculation Against CSM, Yellow Fever, Hepatitis B&nbsp;&nbsp; On 5 January, Ghanaweb reported that the superintendent of the Tema Port Health Unit called on the public to inoculate themselves against cerebrum spinal meningitis, CSM, because it is prevalent in the dry season.&nbsp;&nbsp;Superintendent Saka stated that the unit has enough vaccines in stock for CSM, yellow fever, and hepatitis B and urged the public to avail themselves of the opportunity at an affordable cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;Saka revealed that, in 2007, the Port Health Unit recorded a total of 897 inoculations against CSM, 2,673 against yellow fever, and 358 against Hepatitis B; this compared to figures of 324, 2,710, and 117 respectively in 2006.&nbsp;&nbsp;Explaining his eagerness to see people inoculated, Mr Saka stated that CSM is prevalent in the dry season.&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, hepatitis B, which affects the liver, gradually is gaining ground with people.&nbsp;&nbsp;Hence, it is better to inoculate for protection rather than to wait, become infected, and end up suffering and spending more.&nbsp;&nbsp;The head of the Health Unit assured correspondents that, concerning consumables, his outfit has destroyed large quantities of unwholesome food items worth billions of cedis since the beginning of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is to prevent them from being brought into the local market to be sold to unsuspecting consumers.&nbsp;&nbsp;These unwholesome items were not cleared on time. [(Internet) Ghanaweb WWW-Text in English -- independent web portal; URL: http://www.ghanaweb.com ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Cape Verde: Health Authorities Take Precautions Against Yellow Fever in Brazil&nbsp;&nbsp; Inforpress reported on 11 January that health authorities are not concerned about the outbreak of yellow fever that has put some states of Brazil on standby, as they stated that they have been taking the usual precautions against the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;They added that airplanes are being disinfected, even though Ceara, where Cape Verdian planes land, is not one of the Brazilian regions affected by the outbreak.&nbsp;&nbsp;Health official Jose da Rosa stated that the authorities also are considering introducing the vaccine against this disease as part of those usually given on the archipelago.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, the vaccination is obligatory for those traveling. [Praia Agencia Noticiosa de Cabo Verde (Internet Version-WWW) in Portuguese -- Government-controlled press agency; Internet: http://www.inforpress.cv/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; DRC: Authorities Register Cholera Outbreak in Katanga Province&nbsp;&nbsp; On 1 January, Digital Congo FM reported the outbreak of a cholera epidemic in Bukama and Kamina in Katanga Province.&nbsp;&nbsp;Out of 200 cases recorded, the epidemic is reported to have claimed 11 lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;To date, the Methodist Church has been taking care of the patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;Two trucks loaded with medicines have been sent to Bukama and Kamina. [Kinshasa Digital Congo FM in French -- radio station owned by Multimedia Corporation Congo, a pro-President Kabila company]&nbsp;&nbsp; East Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Somalia: Mysterious Disease Kills Three in Lower Shabelle Region&nbsp;&nbsp; Radio Shabeelle reported on 4 January that several people have died and 20 others are ill following the outbreak of a mysterious disease in a village in the Afgooye District of the Lower Shabeelle Region in southern Somalia.&nbsp;&nbsp; Correspondent Hasan Miyow Hasan reports that three people have died and 20 others are suffering from a mysterious disease that has broken out in Jambalul village of the Afgooye District.&nbsp;&nbsp;The disease, which has claimed lives and affected others, is reported to present with a high fever that has never been experienced before, according Afgooye Health Service head Dr Nur Abdi Abdille. [Mogadishu Radio Shabeelle in Somali --Privately owned, commercial FM station with sister station in Marka, about 50 miles southwest of Mogadishu]&nbsp;&nbsp; Sudan: Northern Darfur State Announces Polio Vaccination Campaign Completion&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Sudanese Media Center reported on 3 January that Northern Darfur State announced that it has completed the vaccination of children against polio with a 100-percent success rate.&nbsp;&nbsp;Abdallah Bashir, the head of the immunization office in the state, stated that the campaign has covered all the state's localities in collaboration with the Federal Ministry of Health, UNICEF, and the WHO.&nbsp;&nbsp;Bashir noted that: "the state could be declared free of polio next year." [Khartoum Sudanese Media Centre (Internet Version-WWW) in Arabic -- online Sudanese news site with Arabic, English, and French versions; http://www.smc.sd ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Uganda: Authorities Investigate Possible Ebola Outbreak in Bundibugyo District&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Kampala The Monitor reported on 6 January that the discovery of eight dead monkeys in the Rwenzori National Park in the Bundibugyo District of western Uganda has raised more fears and tension among locals.&nbsp;&nbsp;The latter are just coming to terms with the Ebola outbreak that ravaged the area and is stated to have been brought to the area by infected monkeys.&nbsp;&nbsp; District leaders and the health workers suspect that the monkeys may have been suffering from another virus, but they are undertaking investigations all the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;A meeting of medical experts and district leaders was held at the district headquarters on 3 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;This was aimed at assessing and fighting the Ebola epidemic, the epicenter of which was in the area.&nbsp;&nbsp;It was noted that the monkeys and chimpanzees have certain blood viruses similar to those detected in human blood samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, the investigations will take cognizance of the fact that the illegal hunting and killing of monkeys in the Mt Rwenzori ranges is continuing uninterrupted.&nbsp;&nbsp;It was agreed that the district authorities of Bundibugyo alert the Uganda Wildlife Authority to intervene with immediate effect.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is because a good number of families living in the mountains are eating monkey meat and putting their lives at risk.&nbsp;&nbsp; Meanwhile, on 4 January Dr Sam Zaramba, the director general of health services, issued a statement stating that the cumulative total of Ebola patients stands at 149 with 37 deaths.&nbsp;&nbsp;"In Bundibugyo District, five people are admitted for treatment currently, one in the Kikyo Health Center and four in Bundibugyo Hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;Of these, one new suspected case has been registered and admitted to Bundibugyo Hospital within the last 24 hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;One patient was discharged from Kikyo Health Center," the statement added.&nbsp;&nbsp;The statement also has positive news, stating that 441 out of 771 contacts have completed 21 days of follow-up and are considered safe.&nbsp;&nbsp;Dr Zaramba cautioned against "creating unnecessary panic." [Kampala The Monitor (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- independent daily owned by the Kenya-based Nation Media Group. URL is http://www.monitor.co.ug/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Uganda: 'Mysterious' Disease Kills Disputed Number of People in Kamwenge District&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Kampala The Monitor reported on 9 January that a mysterious disease with symptoms similar to Marburg hemorrhagic fever has hit the Kamwenge District in western Uganda, killing at least 28 people, according to a local council official on 8 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Justus Nkunda, a councilor in Kabingo Parish in Nkoma Sub-County, told The Monitor that the disease that broke out in his area about a month earlier is highly contagious and is characterized by fever, bleeding, and small swellings.&nbsp;&nbsp;"The disease is not known yet, but 28 people in my area have died already.&nbsp;&nbsp;I pray that the government comes in to investigate before the matter gets out of hand," Mr Nkunda appealed.&nbsp;&nbsp; District Health Officer Dr Sarah Kasewa and District Chairman Mr Edward Musingye have downplayed the outbreak, confirming that only two people, including a nine-month-old baby, have died to date.&nbsp;&nbsp;They suspect malaria to be the cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;"We have not traced anything like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;Only two people have died because of malaria, the handling of which was delayed," stated Mr Musingye.&nbsp;&nbsp; Sudan: Diplomat Warns 'False' Rift Valley Fever Reports Cause Economy To Suffer&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The website of the Sudan Tribune reported on 12 January that a Sudanese diplomat stated false reports about cases of Rift Valley Fever among Sudanese livestock has caused substantial losses of nearly $1 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;Idris Suleiman, deputy head of mission at the Sudanese Embassy in Egypt, stated that unfounded reports related to cases of Rift Valley Fever among Sudanese livestock have caused huge losses to the national economy that have reached nearly $1 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;He added that the losses were incurred by both the private and the public sector.&nbsp;&nbsp;The disease caused a row about its cause between the Ministry of Health, which warned people against eating meat, and the Ministry of Animal Resources, which claimed there is no sign of Rift Valley Fever among livestock.&nbsp;&nbsp; The deadly virus can be transmitted to humans by the handling of animal tissue during slaughtering or butchering, helping with animal births, and in veterinary procedures.&nbsp;&nbsp;Human infections also can result from mosquito bites and consuming meat from infected animals or their milk that is uncooked or not pasteurized milk, according to the WHO.&nbsp;&nbsp; Recently, the export of some 20,000 camels has been banned at the Sudanese border with Egypt as a measure against the disease spreading.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Sudanese authorities denied that these animals carry the infection.&nbsp;&nbsp;"The animals have been at the border for over 40 days now, while the disease causes death in less than five days," stated Idris.&nbsp;&nbsp;At the end of December 2007, the World Health Organization warned that Rift Valley Fever has spread in Sudan rapidly with 218 fatalities and 631 infections since its outbreak in October.&nbsp;&nbsp;With its 150 million head of livestock, Sudan occupies first place among African countries in animal husbandry. [Paris Sudan Tribune WWW-Text in English -- privately owned website; URL: http://www.sudantribune.com ]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HEALTH CARE INFRASTRUCTURE&nbsp;&nbsp; Southern Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Hwange District Hospital Remains Unused Three Years After Completion&nbsp;&nbsp; On 7 January, The Herald Online reported that Hwange District Hospital, which is a few kilometers outside the coal mining town, is yet to open its doors to patients three years after it was completed.&nbsp;&nbsp;The institution was built by the government and is expected to complement other health institutions in serving residents of the town, as well as the surrounding rural communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, three years have passed and the hospital is not operational yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;Hwange does not have a district hospital and patients rely on the privately owned Hwange Colliery Hospital and the St Patrick's and St Mary's Hospitals run by the Catholic Church.&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Chitungwiza Central Hospital Recruits Departmental Heads&nbsp;&nbsp; On 9 January, The Herald Online reported that Chitungwiza Central Hospital has recruited several departmental heads to help improve service delivery as part of the hospitals' strategic plan for 2008.&nbsp;&nbsp;Hospital Chief Executive Dr Obadiah Moyo stated that the managers are expected to make sure that all hospital sections operate properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;"Now, we have set divisions so that the hospital is lined up with other central hospitals.&nbsp;&nbsp;The divisions, which we have labeled 'firms' since, will be chaired by consultants and specialists," stated Dr Moyo.&nbsp;&nbsp;"These include gynecologists, physicians, pediatricians, dermatologists, and pathologists among other specialties.&nbsp;&nbsp;The move is meant to ensure that all categories are run appropriately and efficiently, in order to provide the correct services."&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: German NGO To Fund Establishment of Drug Rehabilitation, Child Care Facility&nbsp;&nbsp; On 11 January, BuaNews Online reported that a German non-governmental organization will invest an amount of 10 million rand for the establishment of a drug rehabilitation and child care facility in Delft, Cape Town.&nbsp;&nbsp;This investment comes after Cape Town Metro Mayor Helen Zille signed a memorandum of understanding with German non-governmental organization Power Child in Germany on 10 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;The invested amount will be used to construct sporting and medical facilities that specifically will be designed to care for children affected by drug addiction, abuse, or abandonment. [Pretoria BuaNews Online WWW-Text in English -- South African Government News Service published by the Government Communication and Information System; Internet: http://www.buanews.goz.za/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Central/West Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Cape Verde: Neves, Luxembourg Prime Minister Open New Hospital on Santiago Island&nbsp;&nbsp; Lusa reported on 14 January that about 120,000 people will benefit from a new hospital on the island of Santiago, which was inaugurated in Santa Catarina by Prime Minister Jose Maria Neves and Luxembourg Prime Minister Jean-Claude Junker on 14 January.&nbsp;&nbsp;The new facility cost over 5 million euros, and the funds were provided by Cape Verde and Luxembourg.&nbsp;&nbsp;The hospital will take the pressure off the only hospital on Santiago and the hospital in Praia.&nbsp;&nbsp; Burkina Faso: Government To Monitor Private Health Centers More Closely&nbsp;&nbsp; Sidwaya reported on 14 January that the government decided on 9 January that: "the development of private health care centers is going to be monitored more closely by the public authorities."&nbsp;&nbsp;This especially concerns the capital Ouagadougou.&nbsp;&nbsp;Thus, the government has decided to "clean up" the private clinic sector.&nbsp;&nbsp;The daily commends the government for this decision, because it has been noticed that such infrastructure is appearing in a rather unorganized manner and it is unclear whether the authorities know what is happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;The daily adds that it is no secret that anybody could stand up and create a clinic just for the sake of profit-making at the moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;Under these conditions, the fate of the patients is not their concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;In a bid to protect the population, the health minister is invited to publish the list of faulty private clinics regularly.&nbsp;&nbsp;There is much hope that this move is just the beginning, because many other sectors need refreshing. [Ouagadougou Sidwaya (Internet Version-WWW) in French -- state-owned, government-controlled daily; URL: http://www.sidwaya.bf ]&nbsp;&nbsp; East Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Somalia: Mogadishu Administration Agrees To Build Health Care Center for Displaced&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Shabeelle Media Network reported on 10 January that the Mogadishu Regional Administration has decided to suspend its previous order to remove all the displaced people from the former Mogadishu Technical Institute premises.&nbsp;&nbsp;After a meeting between representatives of the displaced people and the Mogadishu Regional Administration, it has been agreed to suspend the removal of the displaced people.&nbsp;&nbsp;The regional administration also has agreed to build a health care center for children and mothers, as well as a school, on the premises.&nbsp;&nbsp;Abas Muhammad Nur Galeyr, the information director of the Mogadishu Regional Administration, told the press that the displaced people have been allowed to continue living in the premises of this former technical school. [Mogadishu Shabeelle Media Network (Internet Version-WWW) in Somali Mogadishu Shabeelle Media Network in Somali -- Internet site of independent FM radio and television network based in Mogadishu; Network claims an audience of more than 1.8 million; target audience includes Somalis in-country, diaspora and Somalis in Kenya, Ethiopia, and Djibouti, policymakers working with international organizations, and the UN. Site has partnership with Radio France International. Internet: http://www.shabellenews.com ]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OTHER MEDICAL RELATED ITEMS&nbsp;&nbsp; Southern Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Zambia: Hospitals Treat 800 Mufulira Residents Affected by Water Pollution&nbsp;&nbsp; On 4 January, the Times of Zambia reported that: "about 800 people in Mufulira were treated at two hospitals for stomach and other complications after they consumed water supplied from the Mopani Copper Mines underground source."&nbsp;&nbsp;In an interview, Copperbelt Minister Mwansa Mbulakulima stated that 500 people were treated at Ronald Ross, while close to 300 were attended to at the Malcolm Watson Mine Hospitals as the government constituted a team to establish the cause of the pollution. [Lusaka Times of Zambia (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Government-owned daily; Internet: http://www.times.co.zm/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Angola: Health Minister Visits Ethiopia To Take Part in Extreme Need Conference&nbsp;&nbsp; According to ANGOP on 7 January, Health Minister Ruben Sicato has left for Ethiopia, where he will be taking part in the First International Conference on the Delegation of Duties in Case of Extreme Need.&nbsp;&nbsp;He told the media that matters such as human resources, health systems, and the fight against HIV/AIDS will be discussed.&nbsp;&nbsp;He added that this is a joint initiative of the World Health Organization, the UN Development Fund, and UNAIDS.&nbsp;&nbsp;There will be a US aid program to help less developed countries to fight AIDS, and the minister stated that: "it is necessary to draw up strategies, especially for the delegation of duties, and thus the need for this meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;The challenges are enormous, and experts and analysts, who can conduct proper analytical studies on the disease, are not available always.&nbsp;&nbsp;Hence, it is necessary to train staff and delegate duties," he added. [Luanda ANGOP WWW-Text in Portuguese -- Government-controlled news agency; Internet: http://www.angolapress-angop.ao/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Foreign Currency Shortage Leads to Drug Shortages at Pharmacies&nbsp;&nbsp; On 9 January, The Herald Online reported that: "at least 50 percent of medical drugs in private pharmacies are out of stock owing to foreign currency shortages, while the few available are beyond the reach of most Zimbabweans now."&nbsp;&nbsp;On 8 January, Mr Ishe Nkomo, the president of the Pharmaceutical Society of Zimbabwe, stated that the situation was likely to see many patients failing to access prescribed drugs.&nbsp;&nbsp;Stalenev 30, an anti-retroviral drug commonly used by people living with HIV for first-line treatment, is in short supply and costs an average of 85 million Zimbabwe dollars for a month-long course now.&nbsp;&nbsp;For drugs used for other chronic illnesses such as high blood pressure and diabetes, patients have to pay between 18 million Zimbabwe dollars and 25 million Zimbabwean dollars a course now.&nbsp;&nbsp;The drug costs seem to be pushing medical care far beyond the reach of many, including white-collar workers who take home less than 100 million Zimbabwean dollars every month.&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Nkomo stated that private pharmacies also were being affected by foreign currency shortages.&nbsp;&nbsp; Zimbabwe: Japan Funds Acquisition of 200,000 Long-Lasting Treated Mosquito Nets&nbsp;&nbsp; The Herald Online reported on 10 January that: "pregnant women and children under five years of age in seven areas where malaria is endemic will receive about 200,000 long-lasting treated mosquito nets acquired through a $1.9 million grant from the Japanese Government."&nbsp;&nbsp;The beneficiaries include 150,000 children under five years of age and more than 50,000 pregnant women.&nbsp;&nbsp;Speaking at the signing ceremony for the grant, Dr Festo Kavishe, United Nations Children's Fund country representative, stated that the funding also is expected to boost the procurement of seven routine vaccines for immunization.&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: Private Hospitals Agree To Reconsider Tariff Increases&nbsp;&nbsp; On 11 January, BuaNews Online reported that private hospitals have committed themselves to reconsidering the tariff increases that they have implemented in their facilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;Their promise to reevaluate their decision follows Health Minister's Manto Tshabalala-Msimang's 4 January request to them to reconsider their increases.&nbsp;&nbsp;The meeting, which was attended by the Private Hospital Group chief executives, some health members of executive councils, and 22 representatives from various private hospital groups, sought to discuss concerns surrounding the excessive increase in private hospitals tariffs for 2008, "which are well above the inflation rate."&nbsp;&nbsp; Angola: WHO Congratulates Government on Health System Improvement&nbsp;&nbsp; Jornal de Angola reported on 11 January that Fatoumata Diallo, the representative of the World Health Organization in Angola, congratulated the government on the improvements made to the national health system over the last three years.&nbsp;&nbsp;After a meeting with Parliamentary Chairman Roberto de Almeida, she stated that she has seen many improvements in the health system and other sectors since her arrival.&nbsp;&nbsp;Diallo pointed to the advances that have been made in epidemiological vigilance and in the fight against malaria, TB, and HIV/AIDS.&nbsp;&nbsp;She added that she also noted improvements in mother-and-child care and environmental health, as well as in the treatment of epidemics, such as Marburg, cholera, and polio.&nbsp;&nbsp;Diallo will end her three-year Angola tour in January. [Maputo Jornal de Angola (Internet Version-WWW) in Portuguese -- Government-controlled daily newspaper; Internet: http://www.jornaldeangola.com ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Shortage of Supplies Halts Work at Hospitals in Maputo&nbsp;&nbsp; Media Fax reported on 11 January that a lack of supplies at hospitals in Maputo Province is causing patients to accuse hospital personnel of negligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;The most serious cases were registered at the General Hospital of Machava and at the Dlavela Medical Center, where work was stopped due to a shortage of supplies such as gloves and anesthetics.&nbsp;&nbsp;Patients complain that they are being forced to obtain drugs at private pharmacies, hospital staff members arrive late for work, and some toilets also are not hygienic.&nbsp;&nbsp; RSA: Study Reveals Lack of Nurses With ICU Skills Putting Patients at Risk&nbsp;&nbsp; On 14 January, Business Day reported that: "hospitals are grappling with an acute shortage of nurses with the skills to work in intensive care units, ICUs, posing risks to patients and driving up health-care costs, according to a national audit reported in the latest South African Medical Journal."&nbsp;&nbsp;"Our nurses are tired, often not healthy, and plagued by discontent and low morale," wrote the researchers.&nbsp;&nbsp;"The quality of the training and continuing medical education is dubious, and there are no effective recruitment and retention strategies," they stated.&nbsp;&nbsp;The audit, covering all of South Africa's public and private hospitals, was commissioned by the Critical Care Society of South Africa, representing doctors and nurses working in ICU and high-care units.&nbsp;&nbsp;The researchers found that three-fourths of the 448 ICU nursing managers are ICU-trained nurses, but only one-fourth of the nurses working under them have specialized training.&nbsp;&nbsp;A mere 3.8% of the nurses were trained as neonatal ICU nurses to care for very young babies.&nbsp;&nbsp;Only 5.7% of ICU nurses have more than 20 years of experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;There were 4,168 ICU and high-care beds in South Africa serviced by 4,584 professional nurses, or 1.1 nurses per bed.&nbsp;&nbsp;Using an ideal ratio of three nurses per bed, the authors calculated South Africa has an ICU nurse deficit of nearly 8,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;The "gold standard" should be six to seven nurses per ICU bed and 3.9 per high-care bed. [Johannesburg Business Day (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- privately-owned regional newspaper; Internet: http://www.bday.co.za/ ]&nbsp;&nbsp; Mozambique: Health Authorities Spend $4 Million Annually on Patient Transfers Abroad&nbsp;&nbsp; Media Fax reported on 15 January that health authorities spend over $4 million per annum to pay for the costs of transferring 600 patients abroad, as well as on their treatment.&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Health Ministry, most of the patients requiring treatment abroad suffer from illnesses linked to ophthalmology and various cancers.&nbsp;&nbsp;Portugal and South Africa mainly receive these patients, besides other European countries and the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp; Central/West Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Chad: First Lady Visits New Mothers, Women Suffering With Fistula&nbsp;&nbsp; On 1 January, Ndjamena Radio Nationale Tchadienne reported that First Lady Hinda Deby visiting the Abeche Hospital in a bid to bring comfort to new mothers and to women suffering with fistula.&nbsp;&nbsp;Flanked by the hospital director and the coordinator of the National Program Against Fistula, the first lady visited the hospital's maternity unit to congratulate women who delivered during the night of 31 December.&nbsp;&nbsp;On the occasion, she received the appreciation of Mrs Fatime Malloum, the head of the maternity unit, who seized the opportunity to thank her for her constant availability for preserving human lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;Then First Lady Hinda Deby Itno visited women suffering from fistula who have been operated by Dr Koyalta Mahamat, coordinator of the National Program Against Fistula.&nbsp;&nbsp;Since 20 December, he has been leading a caravan to treat fistula and has operated on 30 patients already.&nbsp;&nbsp;He pointed out that women suffering from fistula come from all over the country and the youngest is 13.&nbsp;&nbsp;Some of them have been operated on two, three, or even four times.&nbsp;&nbsp;Hinda Deby Itno handed over presents as well as cash and asked God to heal them. [Ndjamena Radio Nationale Tchadienne in French -- State-owned radio station]&nbsp;&nbsp; Guinea: Conakry CNTS Director Expresses Concern Over Blood Donation Shortages&nbsp;&nbsp; On 13 January, Le Diplomate reports on the great need for blood donations in the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;The report added that the National Center for Blood Transfusion, CNTS, and its branches all around the country need about 92,000 batches of blood every year to assure the availability of blood for at least 1 percent of the 9 million Guineans.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, Conakry CNTS Director Dr Andre Loua is reported to have stated on 11 January that they still are far from that objective, because they barely collect 15,000 to 20,000 batches per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;He added that the reason behind this poor result is that blood donation is not really a culture in most of the Guinean regions.&nbsp;&nbsp;Many Guineans state that they do not want to give their blood, because they do not have enough to eat.&nbsp;&nbsp;Others fear being tested HIV-positive.&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, the health infrastructure in the country is unfit for the security of the blood, even if it is collected.&nbsp;&nbsp;There is a serious lack of human, material, and financial resources for large-scale awareness campaigns on behalf of blood donation.&nbsp;&nbsp;Support from the media also appears rarely.&nbsp;&nbsp;Nevertheless, Dr Loua assured the public that, at least at the Conakry CNTS, the entire blood donation process is secure.&nbsp;&nbsp;The CNTS, which was created in 1998, is situated inside the Donka Hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;It has a building built with money from the European Union and punctually is sponsored by partners such as the WHO, the World Bank, and other donors.&nbsp;&nbsp;Dr Loua thanked the latter for their great support, which helped take care of the numerous people injured during the January-February 2007 riots.&nbsp;&nbsp;Finally, Dr Loua expressed the hope that with increased financial means splinter centers will open in the countryside and campaigns will be held to help Guineans understand how important it is to donate blood. [Conakry Le Diplomate (Internet Version-WWW) in French -- privately owned weekly newspaper; URL: http://www.lediplomateguinee.info ]&nbsp;&nbsp; East Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; Somalia: Parents Warn Some Children Born During Mogadishu Fighting Suffer Unknown 'Diseases'&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Shabeelle Media Network reported on 8 January that some children born during the fighting in Mogadishu have been seen with unknown diseases.&nbsp;&nbsp;Parents of these children have stated that local doctors cannot deal with these diseases.&nbsp;&nbsp;Faduma Usman, a mother of a six-month-old baby with lacerations, stated that gunpowder was causing many problems for unborn children.&nbsp;&nbsp;Faduma told Shabeelle Radio that her baby girl was born with a swollen body, and her body has developed lacerations day by day since then.&nbsp;&nbsp;She added that the baby also has a problem of dry eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;"I believe my baby's problems were caused by poison from the weapons used in Mogadishu.&nbsp;&nbsp;As you also can see, my baby's father Muhammad Hasan is disabled.&nbsp;&nbsp;We used to live in the Suuqa Xoolaha locality in Mogadishu, before we moved to the Lafoole area [close to the Afgooye District in the Lower Shabeelle Region].&nbsp;&nbsp;Because of poverty, we could not flee as soon as our neighbors did, and only did so when we received help from relatives."&nbsp;&nbsp; Sudan: Al-Bashir Praises Khartoum State Health Insurance System&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of Sudanese news agency Suna reported that Field Marshal Umar al-Bashir, president of the republic, has reaffirmed the commitment of the Government of National Unity to the Comprehensive Peace Agreement, and called on holders of arms in Darfur to join the peace process.&nbsp;&nbsp;Addressing a huge mass rally in the Peace Square of the Sharq al-Nil Locality in Khartoum State on 8 January, the president stated that Sudan wants to solve all its problems, in order to pave the way for implementing development.&nbsp;&nbsp; While addressing a celebration for the launching of 500,000 health insurance cards in Khartoum State as part of the country's celebration of Peace Day, the president lauded the efforts of Khartoum State in the field of health insurance, stating that the experience of health insurance in Sudan that covers all sectors has become a model for social solidarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;The celebration also was addressed by Khartoum State Governor Dr Isma'il al-Mutaafi, who pointed out that the comprehensive health insurance coverage project comes as part of the government's support to the poor.&nbsp;&nbsp;He lauded the institutions that worked to make the project a success, namely the Zakat Chamber, the Ministry of Finance of the state, and the Students Support Fund. [Khartoum Suna news agency (Internet Version-WWW) in English -- Sudanese news agency]&nbsp;&nbsp; Sudan: NGO Warns Infant Mortality Rates in South Sudan Remain High&nbsp;&nbsp; The website of the Sudan Tribune reported on 8 January that British-based international charity Save the Children stated that rates of children mortality remain high in southern Sudan.&nbsp;&nbsp;It also warned that thousands of children still are facing death.&nbsp;&nbsp;Since the signing of the Comprehensive Peace Agreement three years ago, health care conditions for children have reached crisis point.&nbsp;&nbsp;One in four children in Southern Sudan dies before the age of five, mainly from easily preventable diseases such as malaria or diarrhea, according to Save the Children.&nbsp;&nbsp;It added that tens of thousands of families still have very limited access to basic health care, with just one trained doctor for every 100,000 people.&nbsp;&nbsp; The 2005 peace agreement promised to direct the proceeds from Sudan's rich oil reserves to help rebuild the country's destroyed health and education systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;Yet, three years later, communities struggling to survive after the country's 20-year civil war have seen little change.&nbsp;&nbsp;The international charity called on the Southern Sudan Government to commit more funding to strengthening education, health, and protection services.&nbsp;&nbsp;It also urged fundraisers to give more money and advice to the South Sudanese Government to protect children.&nbsp;&nbsp; In a report released at the end of December, the United Nations Children's Fund stated that the number of children who die before their fifth birthday fell below 10 million in 2006, but much more still needs to be done.&nbsp;&nbsp;The UNICEF report attributed the progress in children's mortality rates largely to improvements in health care.&nbsp;&nbsp;By comparison, an estimated 20 million children under five were dying every year at the beginning of the 1960s.&nbsp;&nbsp;However, UNICEF Executive Director Ann Veneman pointed out that: "much more must be done" and "if we do so, we can help create a better world for girls and boys and for generations to come."&nbsp;&nbsp;The study also found an appalling lack of basic sanitation, hygiene, and drinkable water, which contributes to the deaths of more than 1.5 million children from diarrhea and related ailments each year. [Paris Sudan Tribune WWW-Text in English -- privately owned website; URL: http://www.sudantribune.com ]&nbsp;&nbsp;THIS REPORT MAY CONTAIN COPYRIGHTED MATERIAL.&nbsp;&nbsp;COPYING AND DISSEMINATION IS PROHIBITED WITHOUT PERMISSION OF THE COPYRIGHT OWNERS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Inquiries regarding use may be directed to NTIS, US Dept. of Commerce; Publish Date: 20080117; Article ID: AFP20080117601002. Database Contributor: Media Africa. Database Contributor ID: FP20080117601002. Language: English. Accession Number: FP20080117601002.</p>
