TY  - JOUR
TI  - Tanzania: Refugees Cause Huge Environmental 'Damages'
DA  - 2007/01/01/
PY  - 2007
DP  - EBSCOhost
UR  - https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=awn&AN=AFP20071114554015&site=ehost-live
DB  - awn
N1  - <p>Note: Tanzania: Refugees Cause Huge Environmental ,Damages,&nbsp;&nbsp; AN assessment on the impact of refugees on the environment in Tanzania revealed massive destruction of water sources and forest reserves, the House heard yesterday.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The report also disclosed illegal hunting and fishing activities in refugee hosting regions of Kigoma and Kagera. Deputy Minister for Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation Cyril Chami, said the assessment was conducted by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) at the government's request.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; He said the assessment whose report dubbed "Impact of Refugees and Internally Displaced Persons on the Environment in Tanzania", was unveiled in 2005.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; It was done between October and November 2003. The Deputy Minister was answering Dr Chrisant Mzindikaya (CCM -Kwela) who wanted to know if the assessment had been done and what had been the outcome.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dr Chami said despite the destruction caused on the environment, the government was proud to have hosted the refugees and playing a part to ensure their survival.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; "The people we hosted were victims of circumstances and being a good and kind nation we had to welcome them," said the deputy minister in response to a supplementary question posed by Mr George Simbachawene (CCM-Kibakwe).&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mr Simbachawene wanted to know if the government was compensated by the United Nations for hosting the refugees. Dr Chami said the government did not ask for any compensation.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[Description of Source: Dar es Salaam Daily News in English -- http://www.dailynews-tsn.com/]&nbsp;&nbsp;THIS REPORT MAY CONTAIN COPYRIGHTED MATERIAL.&nbsp;&nbsp;COPYING AND DISSEMINATION IS PROHIBITED WITHOUT PERMISSION OF THE COPYRIGHT OWNERS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Inquiries regarding use may be directed to NTIS, US Dept. of Commerce; Publish Date: 20071116; City Source: DAR; Article ID: AFP20071114554015. Database Contributor: Media Africa. Database Contributor ID: AFP20071114554015. Database Subset: African Studies. Language: English. Accession Number: AFP20071114554015.</p>
