N1  - <p>Note: The head of UNMIL says the reintegration of ex-combatants remains a major problem despite the success made in demobilizing thousands of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr. Alan Doss said there is still a job to do in making sure that ex-combatants all over the country find alternatives.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr. Doss spoke on Friday in River Gee County where he led a high power delegation to assess the security situation at the Ivorian border.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Superintendent of River Gee told the delegation that ninety percent of ex-combatants in the County did not go through the disarmament program.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr. Christian Chea said most of these ex-combatants have turned into what he called illegal hunters roaming the forest.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr. Chea however, dismissed rumors that there were still assault weapons in River Gee and secret training of individuals to fight in Ivory Coast.&nbsp;&nbsp; [Description of Source: (Internet) Star Radio WWW-Text in English -- Internet version of the independent Star Radio station; root URL as of filing date: http://www.hirondelle.org/sites]&nbsp;&nbsp;THIS REPORT MAY CONTAIN COPYRIGHTED MATERIAL.&nbsp;&nbsp;COPYING AND DISSEMINATION IS PROHIBITED WITHOUT PERMISSION OF THE COPYRIGHT OWNERS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Inquiries regarding use may be directed to NTIS, US Dept. of Commerce; Publish Date: 20060220; City Source: (INTERNET); Article ID: AFP20060220636019. Database Contributor: Media Africa. Database Contributor ID: FP20060220636019. Language: English. Accession Number: FP20060220636019.</p>
