N1  - <p>Note: KINSHASA, Sept 29 (AFP) - Members of Uganda's notorious Lord's Resistance Army (LRA) are terrorizing civilians and killing wildlife in the restive eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, DRC officials said Thursday.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A band of LRA fighters led by the group's deputy chief Vincent Otti that fled to the DRC earlier this month have terrified the local population and have begun poaching elephants in Garamba National Park, they said.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; "The LRA rebels who have installed themselves on the edges of the park have started poaching," park director Paulin Tshikaya told AFP. "On Monday, they killed two elephants."&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; "The situation is catastrophic," he said, adding that conservationists feared the LRA would expand their illegal hunting operations to include other rare and endangered animals in the park.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; "We have a unique species of white rhinoceros there of which there are only about 10 left," Tshikaya said. "Most of these animals have been killed by poachers over the years and could disappear completely if the rebels stay there."&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A Garamba-based official with the Congolese Institute for the Conservation of Nature (ICCN) confirmed the poaching incidents and said the rebels were also assaulting the residents of the town of Aba just outside the park.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; "They are holding the local population hostage," he told AFP on condition of anonymity. "Many Aba residents have fled into the bush. People are scared because of the LRA and Ugandan troops massing on the other side of the border."&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kampala alleges that Otti and his fighters -- which UN officials say number some 380 -- who fled Ugandan military operations in southern Sudan are seeing asylum in the DRC although Kinshasa has denied any such request has been made.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Earlier this week, Uganda demanded that the rebels be deported and the UN mission in the DRC, known as MONUC, said Wednesday it was ready to assist the DRC army in disarming them.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The LRA, which has long maintained bases in the south of Sudan, where Ugandan troops have Khartoum's approval to operate, has waged a devastating rebellion in northern Uganda since 1988.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [Description of Source: Paris AFP (World Service) in English -- world news service of the independent French news agency Agence France Presse]&nbsp;&nbsp;THIS REPORT MAY CONTAIN COPYRIGHTED MATERIAL.&nbsp;&nbsp;COPYING AND DISSEMINATION IS PROHIBITED WITHOUT PERMISSION OF THE COPYRIGHT OWNERS.&nbsp;&nbsp;Inquiries regarding use may be directed to NTIS, US Dept. of Commerce; Publish Date: 20050929; City Source: PARIS AFP (WORLD SERVICE). Database Contributor: Media Africa. Database Contributor ID: iPAD-AHA-001459. Language: English. Accession Number: iPAD-AHA-001459.</p>
