TY  - JOUR
TI  - An ASSET for endangered species and habitats on the north Kenyan coast
AU  - Jackson, C
T2  - Ostrich
AB  - NATCHA Abstract: Arabuko-Sokoke Forest and Mica Creek on the Kenyan coast are home to six globally Endangered bird species and internationally important populations of waterbirds, respectively. Both are designated as 'Important Bird Areas' (IBAs) under the BirdLife International IBA programme and Arabuko-Sokoke Forest is a top-priority forest in Africa for its bird conservation value. Threats to the biodiversity and habitat quality of these sites include logging of trees and mangroves for timber, building poles and firewood, illegal hunting, and fishing with destructive methods such as use of poison and fishing nets with undersized mesh. Often the pressure that drives families to earn a few shillings in this way is the need to pay secondary school fees. A Rocha Kenya has initiated a unique ecotourism community conservation project, 'ASSETS' (Arabuko-Sokoke Schools and Ecotourism Scheme), that provides 'eco-bursaries' to children living adjacent to the forest and creek to attend secondary school with money generated primarily from ecotourism. Since starting in January 2002, 90 children now attend secondary school with ASSETS eco-bursaries and they and their families have received regular direct environmental awareness about the importance of the forest and creek. They have also planted over 9 000 mixed indigenous and exotic trees around their homes and in the primary schools from where the children are selected. Ecotourism facilities, such as a suspended boardwalk through mangroves to a bird hide near a wader roost with Crab Plovers, have been constructed to raise funds for ASSETS. It is planned that ASSETS will eventually include 36 primary schools around the forest and creek, with the aim of directly benefitting the local community from the habitats and thus stimulate an active concern for their conservation
