TY  - JOUR
TI  - Courtesy costs nothing: Hunting and game management - part 2
AU  - Grafton, R N
T2  - Fauna & Flora
AB  - NATCHA Abstract: Have you ever wondered how some lucky people get all the hunting they want? They seem to have the right contacts all over the country and know a host of friendly farmers. You could of course put it down to good fortune, money and plenty of free time. But what of all those happy hunters who work a 52-day week, earn the same as you and I, 2 and don't even know the meaning of the word luck. What is it that gives them the entree to so many hunting opportunities? Obviously there are many factors influencing the availability of hunting. It does help an awful lot to have a friend or a relation who has a farm teeming with game. But, on the other hand, there are more than a few such persons to whom the gates are locked on that farm, while complete strangers are made welcome, gun and all. A couple of weeks ago I was talking to a fellow about where to get some duiker shooting. He told me the sad tale of how last season he'd had a good contact but that this year the farmer had said he would allow no more duiker to be shot. When I heard the details of that previous outing, I wasn't surprised. Apparently this chap had taken a friend with him on the duiker shoot. No, they did not get any duikers but his friend got a steenbuck! Of course the farmer had said nothing about friends, let alone about steenbuck, of which he had only four on the whole farm. These two keen hunters had obviously shot themselves right off their newfound hunting ground. An invitation to one or two persons does not include all their friends, and you need to be seriously colour-blind not to distinguish between a grey duiker and a steenbuck. Of course, you could say the farmer should have made it quite clear - "no friends, no steenbuck". But then he is the host and it is for the guests to tread lightly. It is very sensible always to ask what you may and may not do; that way you can avoid misunderstandings and unpleasantness. There are scores of little do's and don'ts in hunting etiquette but most of them boil down to good manners, common sense and fair play. It is pretty obvious that you do not leave the gates open, set fire to the veld, shoot up sign boards, or send bullets whining over the farm house. Nor do you uproot mealies to make a hide, or damage the few willows on the river bank. I saw a sign once that read: "We enjoy the fishing too, take your litter home with you". I reckon this also applies to empty cartridge cases, plucked feathers and picnic leftovers. It is amazing how an expended cartridge can tell tales on you. That spent case lying beside the tracks of your vehicle, a mile or two from the nearest road, means you don't like walking and are a downright illegal hunter. The odd triple-A empty among the number 8's means you did not stick to rock pigeons. Many deer hunters in the United States just hang around one spot waiting for some one else's shots to scare a buck in their direction. We don't have anything like that hunting pressure here in Transvaal so the fewer shots you fire, the less nervous and jumpy will the game be. This can benefit not only yourself, but also the next hunter to come along
