TY  - JOUR
TI  - Moving past biological determinism in discussions of women and crime during the 1870s-1920s: a note regarding the literature
AU  - Scheider, MC
T2  - DEVIANT BEHAVIOR
AB  - Turn of the century writings on female criminal behavior frequently have been characterized as few in number and biologically deterministic in orientation. Although it is accurate to state that many of these works are overly biologistic, it is incorrect to refer to them only in such a manner. This article reviews writings on women and crime from the 1870s-1920s. Findings reveal that discussions of this issue were commonplace and invoke many of the same theoretical explanations currently used for female criminality (chivalry, opportunity, liberation, and patriarchy). When the biologistic (and sexist) orientations of these writings are removed, we are left with discussions that look strikingly similar to some of today's most popular theoretical statements. The implications of a lack of attention to early criminological work is also discussed.
