AB  - This study investigates personality dimensions in male and female delinquent and non-delinquent samples in the city of Valencia. The dependent variables were four scales from the Extraversion, Neuroticism and Rigidity Questionnaire (ENR); Criminal Propensity Settle from the EPQ-J, and Intelligence from Raven's Progressive Matrices. Results show significant differences in dogmatism, neuroticism, criminal propensity and intelligence, with higher scores for delinquents compared to non-delinquents, except for intelligence where the reverse was found. Neuroticism is found to be the best predictor for discriminating female delinquents from non-delinquents, while intelligence and criminal propensity are good predictors at discriminating both male and female delinquents from non-delinquents. The findings are discussed in the context of Eysenck's theory on crime and personality.
