TI  - Scouse? but not Scouser? Embedded enregistered repertoires for adolescent girls on The Wirral
AU  - Cooper, P
AU  - Lampropoulou, S
T2  - LANGUAGE & COMMUNICATION
AB  - Liverpool English (henceforth LE), also known as ?Scouse1?, is one of the most widely recognised varieties of British English (Montgomery, 2010). There are also many widely-held beliefs about Liverpool and its speakers, which we can see expressed as explicit links between language features and social values that have led to the variety becoming enregistered (Agha, 2003; Honeybone and Watson, 2013). However, many prominent stereotypes of ?Scouse? speakers index (Silverstein, 2003) associations with being urban, working-class, and male, as seen in media representations (Boland, 2008). In addition, we also see representations of ?working-class poverty and [.] the social effects of mass unemployment? (Crowley 2012, p.110) in media representations of Liverpool, as illustrated in TV series set in the city, such as Z-Cars in the 1960s, as well as later shows like The Liver Birds and Brookside. This in turn has led to associations of Scouse with criminality, emotional volatility, and violence (Boland 2008) alongside what Juskan (2018, p.19) describes as the economic and social decline of Liverpool in the latter half of the twentieth century. Perceptions of ?Scouse? amongst younger, middle-class speakers have been largely unexplored although there is some
Liverpool English, or ?Scouse?, is reportedly spreading into surrounding areas including the Wirral. Interviews with adolescent female speakers on the Wirral revealed that certain language features are perceived as sounding more or less ?Scouse?. Some features were also strongly associated with a stereotypical ?Scouser? identity that speakers evaluated negatively and explicitly denied. Others were associated with sounding like a Liverpool speaker and evaluated positively. We conclude that there are multiple and embedded repertoires perceived by these speakers: a ?Scouse? repertoire associated with solidarity, locality (Liverpool) and coolness and the repertoire of a ?Scouser? which is associated with being unintelligible, non-posh and annoying.
Crown Copyright ? 2021 Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
