TI  - When Tolerance Becomes Healthy: Influence of Corruption Tolerance on Psychological Distress among Employees in Nigeria
AU  - Ogungbamila, Bolanle
AU  - Ajagunna, Fisayo Adebayo
T2  - Trends in Psychology
AB  - Previous studies have shown that corruption could have adverse effects on the society, organization and employees as well as the recipients of their services. Corruption tolerance may encourage the pervasiveness of corruption in the workplace, which may comprise the psychological health of employees, especially those that are intolerant of corruption. This study, therefore, tested the extent to which categories of corruption tolerance (low, moderate, and high) influence psychological distress in 400 employees (180 males; 220 females) selected from public and private sector organizations in Lagos, Ibadan, and Abuja, Nigeria. The participants responded to measures of corruption tolerance and psychological distress. Results of the One-way ANOVA and Bonferroni multiple group comparison test showed that corruption tolerance significantly influenced psychological distress; with employees who had low level of corruption tolerance being the most distressed compared to those who moderately tolerated and highly tolerated corruption. The results of an additional analysis indicated that employees who were not married had the significantly lower level of psychological distress compared to those who were married or lost their spouses to death, divorce or separation. The implications of the findings for theory, research and practice are discussed.
