TI  - Are the People Victims or Accomplices? The Use of Presents and Bribes to Influence Officials in Eastern Europe
AU  - Miller, William L
AU  - Grodeland, Ase B
AU  - Koshechkina, Tatyana Y
T2  - Crime, Law and Social Change
AB  - Analysis of 1997/98 interview data from 4,778 people & 1996 focus group & in-depth interview data (N = 26 discussions & 136 people, respectively) from the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Bulgaria, & Ukraine reveal that their differing historical & bureaucratic traditions have led to radically different experiences & perceptions of bribery & victimization. Attitudes about bribery, its moral status, its utility, & who is responsible -- the government or the citizens -- are examined, as well as social realities as seen in census & more direct sociological data. Findings suggest a counterintuitive conclusion: in countries where bribery is least common (Czech Republic), the relatively small number of people who gave bribes were more likely to be accomplices or even corrupters; where bribery was most common (Ukraine), the much larger numbers of people who gave bribes were far more likely to be victims of extortion. 34 Tables. T. Hunt
