AB  - Analyzes how corruption is linked to scandalization in the traditions of European countries & how this has helped shape contemporary perceptions. GB & Italy are contrasted in how they maintained or transmuted patron-client relationships & monitored distinctions between social, economic, & political exchange. The comparative analysis is extended to encompass regional variations within Italy, as manifested by differing levels of trust & tolerance of deviations from public office obligations. Different patterns of monitoring bureaucratic behavior help explain why low-level corruption is less prevalent in France than in Italy, but also why problem consciousness has developed more among Italians. The topographic discussion moves beyond national borders with reference to whether & how European governments responded to international pressures to monitor the bribery of officials abroad. Supplementary indicators of national tendencies to combat corruption are extrapolated from survey data collected on business executives worldwide (N unspecified) about the willingness of national elites to control practices by representatives of their companies in developing countries. 1 Table, 21 References. Adapted from the source document.
