TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Ethical Issue Of International Bribery: A Study Of Atti: JBE
AU  - Longenecker, Justin G
AU  - McKinney, Joseph A
AU  - Moore, Carlos W
T2  - Journal of Business Ethics
AB  - A survey of US business professionals was conducted to assess attitudes surrounding the issue of international bribery.  Subjects were 2,219 individuals classified as being involved in management or in a business profession.  Respondents were asked to rate the personal acceptability of a business payment vignette on a 7-point scale that ranged from one (never acceptable) to 7 (always acceptable).  The results showed that 48.7% of all respondents found the decision unacceptable to some degree, 18.6% perceived the payment to be acceptable to some degree, and 32.5% were classified as neutral.  Four underlying patterns appeared in the respondents' comments: 1.  moralism, 2.  pragmatism, 3.  cultural relativism, and 4.  legalism.  The Foreign Corrupt Practices Act of 1977 clearly established US public policy as disapproving of international bribery.  Some respondents suggested that an international agreement banning bribery in international business dealings is necessary.
