TY  - JOUR
TI  - THE PREVALENCE OF PUBLIC SECTOR CORRUPTION IN SOUTH AFRICA: RELEVANCE OF THE SOCIAL VALUE ECOSYSTEM
AU  - Netswera, Fulufhelo G
AU  - Phago, Kedibone G
T2  - Loyola Journal of Social Sciences
AB  - The link between corruption and low economic development is a long established fact. The World Bank suggests that corruption is responsible for reduced efficiency in the use of public resources and inadvertently increases inequality while the World Economic Forum and the World Bank estimates corruption to cost more than 5% of the global growth domestic product estimated at US$2.6 trillion. Corruption in public sector institutions has reached crisis proportions in many developing countries and South Africa is no exception. South Africa has, in the recent past, been embroiled in corruption scandals estimated at billions of Rands. Transparency International ranked South Africa 45 in the 2016 Corruption Watch Index {where 0 equal highly corrupt and 100 very clean). Over the past few years, it would appear, corruption levels and with it corruption tolerance, seem be on the increase in South Africa. In view of escalating public corruption levels, this article examines whether corruption tolerance is an outcome of changing social and normative practices. The discussion draws on the Social Values Model which provides the eco-system of the value dimensions that are instrumental to the application of societal ethics and the upholding of high moral standards. The discussion posits that group values are an interplay between societal and cultural values, communal values as well as individual values. The discussion bemoans the breakdown of the family structure as a possible contributory factor in the breakdown endowed and rural based municipalities operate in unique conditions that will require unique responses if they are to acquire smart Municipality status.
