AB  - The objective of this study is to develop a multinomial, discrete choice model of household residential location decisions that can be used to determine the effects of locational factors, such as public services, physical attributes, and socio-demographic attributes, and household-level characteristics on the location choice probability of households. The research assumes that homeowners' location decisions are jointly influenced by locational features of neighborhoods and household characteristics and seeks to estimate the relative importance of these two different types of characteristics. In doing so, the goal of the research is to investigate some of the main causal factors of suburbanization and to shed light on the extent to which the suburbanization process observed in the study area is due to life-cycle factors, such as increases in household income and size, vs. lower public service levels and other quality of life attributes of the central city vs. suburbs. Identification of the relative magnitudes of these two effects is an understudied area of the literature and has important policy implications. If the suburbanization process is spurred largely by household-level changes, then changes in public service levels within the central city, such as an increase in public school quality, will not have as large an effect on household movement as otherwise would be expected. A hybrid conditional logit model is used that allows the determination of the relative importance of selected households characteristics versus location specific ii
