TI  - Assessing Abused Women Who Kill: (Ir)relevance of Gender in the Treatment of Spouse-Murder Defendants by the Criminal Justice Systems
AU  - Deb, A
T2  - GENDER EQUITY: CHALLENGES AND OPPORTUNITIES
A2  - Mahajan, V
A2  - Chowdhury, A
A2  - Kaushal, U
A2  - Jariwala, N
A2  - Bong, SA
AB  - The Indian scholarship on female criminality, primarily based on research conducted in correctional homes, reveals that most of the crimes committed by women are due to stressful situations like marital maladjustment, conflict-prone relationship with husband or in scenarios where her/family's subsistence is at stake. However, given the rarity of women's involvement in brutal crimes, extensive studies on the judicial discourse vis-a-vis female murderers have not yet found their place in the existing literature. This paper adds a different voice in highlighting the inherent incapacity of criminal law to engage with experiences of women who kill, in the context of spouse-murder cases, and also contributes to the under-theorized area of judicial response to female crimes in India. The study illuminates how the parallel use of legal defences by men, who kill out of jealousy and anger and abused women, who kill out of fear of violence, is problematic to the extent that they continue to perpetuatemale aggression; and violent subordination of women. The author engages in an analysis of the decisions of the Supreme Court of India and High Courts on spousal homicide, where the offenders have resorted to defence pleas in order to mitigate the punishment. To provide a clear picture of how gender plays a vital role in the disparate treatment of spouse-murder defendants, parallels are drawn between the cases of abused women killers and cases where the law has been used by men to validate their "out-of-jealousy" killings. The author argues that it is untenable to draw a parity between a person who kills out of jealousy or rage and another who acts in self-preservation and to attach the same amount of culpability to both of them. The author ultimately proposes that the law should be made equally available to all those defendants who are similarly situated, irrespective of their gender.
