TY  - CHAP
TI  - Feminist Theories of Criminal Behavior
AU  - Whiteley, Kathryn M.
T2  - The Encyclopedia of Criminology and Criminal Justice
A2  - Albanese, Jay S
AB  - Abstract
            
              Feminist criminology arose in the late 1960s alongside the second wave of feminism. Pioneering feminist criminologists, such as Frances Heidensohn, Dorie Klein, Freda Adler, and Carol Smart, argued that criminology was a study of the male criminal conducted by male criminologists. They further contended that theories of crime studied males and as such were incomplete without the analysis of criminality perpetrated by females. Accomplishments of feminist criminology include the theories of pathways to crime and battered woman syndrome, and the creation of a division of feminist criminology of the American Society of Criminology (along with the esteemed journal
              Feminist Criminology
              ). But perhaps the single most important accomplishment of feminist criminology is that the female offender no longer stands as an afterthought in criminological research.
