TY  - JOUR
TI  - Latent lip print classification using mobilenet for Forensic Investigations
AU  - Vasavi, J.
AU  - Abirami, M.S.
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
AB  - With the help of a lip print, it is feasible to differentiate between forensic and non-forensic cases. Even though numerous pieces of research aimed at recognizing and categorizing lip print patterns have been published, it has not yet been decided which classification of lip prints from large populations and unconstrained scenarios to detect the presence of crimes. In this research paper, we propose an advanced deep learning based optimization framework designed to identify the lip print, an essential component in forensic investigations. Our approach leverages the Large-Scale Multi-Camera Detection dataset. The collected lip prints are preprocessed, feature extracted and then classified using the MobileNet model with residual learning. The simulation is conducted in MATLAB to enhance the robustness of lip print recognition over various performance metrics that include accuracy of 99.6 %, precision of 99.21 %, recall of 99.43 %, and f-measure of 99.31 %. The results show that the proposed method achieves a higher rate of classification accuracy than other methods. Then the results were improved by the Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) technique. © 2024
