AB  - This paper examines the impact of Chinese business managers' moral philosophies on the perception of corrupt payments such as bribery, kickbacks and gift giving. Business managers from Mainland China were selected as target respondents. As hypothesized the survey results generally indicate that moral relativism is a significant predictor of Chinese business managers' favorable perception of bribery and kickbacks. In examining the attitude toward gift giving, the survey showed that an individual's attitude toward gift giving was neither affected by their moral relativism nor by their moral idealism, which implies that gift giving is widely accepted as legal practice in business in Chinese cultural society. © 2007 Springer Science+Business Media B.V.
